\section{Maximum entanglement entropy change for a Pauli gadget on an arbitrary state}

Consider applying a Pauli gadget $e^{-i \phi P/2}$ to an arbitrary pure state $\ket{\psi}$ with entanglement entropy $S_0$ across the bipartition $A|B$, and asking what the maximal change in the entropy is~\cite{Leifer2003, Dur2001, Linden2009, Kraus2001, Chefles2005, Berry2003}. We can first simplify the problem by turning it into a 2-qubit problem. One can transform Pauli strings $P_A$ and $P_B$ in each subsystem into a single $Z$ Pauli operator using Clifford unitaries that act only within each subsystem. Hence, the problem is equivalent to determining the maximum change in entanglement entropy of a 2-qubit state (possibly with ancillas) after applying a phase gadget $e^{-i \phi Z \otimes Z/2}$.

For a 2-qubit pure state $\ket{\psi} = a\ket{00} + b\ket{01} + c\ket{10} + d\ket{11}$, the entanglement entropy is directly related to concurrence $C=2|ad-bc|$ as
\begin{equation}
    S = h_2 \!\left(\frac{1+\sqrt{1-C^2}}{2}\right),
\end{equation}
where $h_2(x)=-x \log x-(1-x)\log(1-x)$ is the binary entropy. Concurrence can be parametrized by angle $C=|\sin(\theta/2)|$, and thus the initial entanglement entropy has a form
\begin{equation}
    S(\theta) = h_2 \!\left(\frac{1+|\cos(\theta/2)|}{2}\right).
\end{equation}

After one applies the phase gadget $e^{-i \phi Z \otimes Z/2}$, the coefficients of the state change as $a\mapsto e^{-i \phi/2}a, b\mapsto e^{i \phi/2}b, c\mapsto e^{i \phi/2}c, d\mapsto e^{-i \phi/2}d$. The concurrence is now
\begin{equation}
    C^2 = 4(|a d|^2+|b c|^2-2|a d|\,|b c|\cos \delta), 
    \quad
    \delta = \arg(ad) - \arg(bc) - 2 \phi.
\end{equation}
Differentiating gives
\begin{equation}
    \Big| \frac{d}{d\phi} C^2 \Big| = 16 |ad|\, |bc|\, |\sin \delta| \le 2 C\sqrt{1-C^2}.
\end{equation}
The inequality can be checked easily using normalization and $2|ad| \le |a|^2+|d|^2, 2|bc| \le |b|^2+|c|^2$. Since $C=|\sin(\theta/2)|$,
\begin{equation}
    \frac{d\theta}{d\phi} = \frac{d(C^2)/d\phi}{C\sqrt{1-C^2}} \le 2.
\end{equation}
Integrating from 0 to $\phi$, gives $|\theta_\phi-\theta_0|\le 2|\phi|$, and hence the phase gadget can move the angle $\theta$ by at most $2|\phi|$.

Hence, the largest possible entropy change is bounded by the largest change over an angular displacement of $2|\phi|$~\cite{Leifer2003},
\begin{equation}
    |\Delta S| \le 
    \max_\theta \Big[ S(\theta+2\phi) - S(\theta) \Big]
    = \max_\theta \left[
        h_2 \!\left(\frac{1+|\cos(\theta/2 + \phi)|}{2}\right)
        - h_2 \!\left(\frac{1+|\cos(\theta/2)|}{2}\right)
    \right] = \Delta S_{\text{max}}.
    \label{eq:generic_entropy_bound}
\end{equation}

\textbf{Infinitesimal entangling rate and ancilla independence.} For small angle $\phi$, we can expand $S(\theta + 2 |\phi|) = S(\theta) + 2 |\phi| S'(\theta) + \mathcal{O}(\phi^2)$, and thus the bound is linear in $\phi$~\cite{Dur2001},
\begin{equation}
    \Delta S_{\text{max}} = 2 |\phi| \max_\theta |S'(\theta)| + \mathcal{O} (\phi^2),
\end{equation}
where $\max_\theta |S'(\theta)| \approx 0.4780683222$. Clifford transformations within subsystems reduce any Pauli gadget crossing the bipartition to a two-qubit interaction, but the remaining qubits may act as entangled ancillas. For self-inverse product Hamiltonians, ancillas provably do not increase the maximal infinitesimal entangling rate~\cite{Wang2003}, extending the linear bound to arbitrary multi-qubit states. Numerical evidence suggests that this ancilla independence persists at finite angles~\cite{Leifer2003}, supporting the extension of Eq.~\eqref{eq:generic_entropy_bound} to multi-qubit states.

\section{Entanglement entropy change for Pauli gadgets on a graph state}

Consider a graph state $\ket{G}$ and a set of Pauli gadgets $U_i = e^{- i \varphi_i P_i / 2}$. The purpose is to determine the change in entanglement entropy after the application of all Pauli gadgets. The graph state $| G \rangle$ with chosen subsystems $A$ and $B$ can always be expressed in the following form:
\begin{equation}
  (C_A \otimes C_B) | G \rangle = | \tilde{G} \rangle = | \Phi_D \rangle_{A_e,
  B_e} \otimes | \mathbf{0} \rangle_{A_l} \otimes | \mathbf{0}
  \rangle_{B_l},
\end{equation}
where $C_A, C_B$ are the Clifford unitaries supported on subsystem $A$ or $B$, $| \Phi_D \rangle$ is a maximally entangled state with entanglement entropy $S_0$ and Schmidt rank $D = 2^{S_0}$, regions $A_e$ and $B_e$ are entangled degrees of freedom (subregions of $A$ and $B$ with exactly $D$ qubits each), and regions $A_l$ and $B_l$ (the rest of $A$ and $B$) are only consisting of $| 0 \rangle$ ``ancillas''. Note that $\ket{\tilde G}$ is not necessarily a graph state, but it is equivalent to one, up to single-qubit Cliffords.

The Clifford unitaries $C_A, C_B$ can also be used to transform all Pauli gadgets $U_i = e^{- i \varphi_i P_i / 2}$,
\begin{equation}
  (C_A \otimes C_B) U_i (C_A \otimes C_B)^{\dag} = (C_A \otimes C_B) e^{- i
  \varphi_i P_i / 2} (C_A \otimes C_B)^{\dag} = e^{- i \varphi_i  \tilde{P}_i
  / 2} .
\end{equation}
In the following, we will drop the tilde from the notation, assuming that we always work with the transformed operators and states. Importantly, the unitary transformations performed here are localised to $A$ and $B$, and therefore do not change the entanglement entropy.

\subsection{One gadget}

We begin by applying one gadget to the graph state,
\begin{align}
  \ket{\psi} & = e^{- i \varphi_1 P_1 / 2} \ket{G}
  \\
  & = \left( \cos \frac{\varphi_1}{2} I - i \sin \frac{\varphi_1}{2} P_1   \right) (| \Phi_D \rangle_{A_e, B_e} \otimes | \mathbf{0} \rangle_{A_l} \otimes | \mathbf{0} \rangle_{B_l})
  \\
  & = \cos \frac{\varphi_1}{2}  | G \rangle - i \sin \frac{\varphi_1}{2} 
  (I \otimes P_1^{B_e} (P_1^{A_e})^T) | \Phi_D \rangle_{A_e, B_e} \otimes
  P_1^{A_l} | \mathbf{0} \rangle_{A_l} \otimes P_1^{B_l} | \mathbf{0}
  \rangle_{B_l}. \label{eq:statedecomp}
\end{align}
The last equality follows from the property of a maximally entangled state, $(A \otimes B) | \Phi_D \rangle = (I \otimes B A^T) | \Phi_D \rangle = (A B^T \otimes I) | \Phi_D \rangle$.

$P_1^{A_l}$ applied on $| \mathbf{0} \rangle_{A_l}$ will either leave the
qubits in $| \mathbf{0} \rangle_{A_l}$ (up to a phase factor) if $P_1^{A_l}$ only consists of $I$ and $Z$, or will change it to an orthogonal state $| \mathbf{x} \rangle_{A_l}$ if $P_1^{A_l}$ consists of $X$ or $Y$, where $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{F}_2^{| A_l |}$.
Similarly, $P_1^{B_l}$ will either leave $| \mathbf{0} \rangle_{B_l}$ or will change it to some orthogonal state $| \mathbf{y} \rangle_{B_l}$.
Hence, the resulting state is a superposition of the following two terms:
\begin{equation}
    P_1^z \ket{G} = \eta_z (I\otimes R_z) \ket{\Phi_D}_{A_e,B_e} \otimes \ket{\mathbf{x}}_{A_l} \otimes \ket{\mathbf{y}}_{B_l},
\end{equation}
where $z \in \{0,1\}$, $\eta_z$ is a phase factor of $\{1,i,-1,-i\}$, $R_{z=0} = I$ and $R_{z=1}=P_1^{B_e} (P_1^{A_e})^T$. Each term is taken with a corresponding weight, $\cos(\phi_1/2)$ or $-i \sin(\phi_1/2)$.

This form allows us to determine all possible changes in entanglement entropy relatively easily. The entropy remains $S_0$ if for $z=1$ bitstrings $\mathbf{x}$ or $\mathbf{y}$ are $\mathbf{0}$ and $R_z$ is Hermitian. If $R_z$ is not Hermitian for $z=1$, then the entropy decreases to $S_0 -1 + h_2(\frac{1}{2}(1-\sin \varphi_1))$, where $h_2(x) = -x \log x - (1-x) \log (1-x)$ is the binary entropy. Finally, if $\mathbf{x}\neq\mathbf{0}$ and $\mathbf{y}\neq\mathbf{0}$ for $z=1$, then the two branches in the superposition are orthogonal, increasing the entanglement entropy to $S_0 + h_2(\sin^2(\varphi_1/2))$.

Therefore, the change of entanglement entropy for one Pauli gadget applied on a graph state can be bounded from above by $S - S_0 \le h_2(\sin^2(\varphi_1/2))$.

\subsection{Two gadgets}

Consider now two gadgets $U_1$ and $U_2$ applied on the graph state.
The resulting state is a superposition of four terms,
\begin{equation}
    P_2^{z_2} P_1^{z_1} \ket{G} = \eta_{\mathbf{z}} (I\otimes R_{\mathbf{z}}) \ket{\Phi_D}_{A_e,B_e} \otimes \ket{\mathbf{x}}_{A_l} \otimes \ket{\mathbf{y}}_{B_l},
\end{equation}
where the bitstrings are $\mathbf{x} = z_1  \mathbf{x}_1 \oplus z_2  \mathbf{x}_2$, $\mathbf{y} = z_1  \mathbf{y}_1 \oplus z_2  \mathbf{y}_2$, $\mathbf{z}=\{z_1, z_2\}$, and $\oplus$ is the addition modulo 2. $\mathbf{x}_i$ is the bitstring after applying $P_i^{A_l}$ on $\ket{\mathbf{x}_i}_{A_l}$, and similarly for $\mathbf{y}_i$, while $\eta_{\mathbf{z}}$ is a phase factor one obtains after transforming the bitstrings.

Calculation of the entropy is now slightly more involved, but can be done by forming a $4D \times4D$ matrix of amplitudes, with submatrices of size $D\times D$ given by
\begin{equation}
  M_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}} = \sum_{\mathbf{z} \,:\, \mathbf{x}
  (\mathbf{z}) = \mathbf{u}, \mathbf{y} (\mathbf{z}) = \mathbf{v}}
  \beta_{\mathbf{z}} R_{\mathbf{z}} ,
\end{equation}
where $\beta_{\mathbf{z}} = \eta_{\mathbf{z}}  (- i)^{\sum_i z_i}  \prod_i
\cos^{1 - z_i} (\varphi_i/2) \sin^{z_i} (\varphi_i/2)$ is the amplitude of the term corresponding to
$\mathbf{z}$ in the superposition. Singular values of the matrix $M$ are, after normalization, the Schmidt coefficients of the final state, $\{\sigma_r(M)/\sqrt{D}\}$, where $\sigma_r(\cdot)$ designates the $r$-th singular value of the matrix. Entanglement can thus be calculated as
\begin{equation}
    S=-\sum_{r=1}^{4D} \frac{\sigma_r(M)^2}{D} \log_2 \frac{\sigma_r(M)^2}{D}.
\end{equation}

We find that for two gadgets, entanglement entropy can have 23 distinct forms. This classification arises because two gadgets generate four stabilizer-state branches, whose interference is constrained by the stabilizer structure and their overlaps across the bipartition. Most can be thought of as a straightforward generalization of the one-gadget results, where the entanglement entropy is bounded from above by an additive bound $S \le S_0 + h_2(\sin^2(\varphi_1/2)) + h_2(\sin^2(\varphi_2/2))$. However, we also find scenarios when this bound is sometimes violated: the Pauli gadgets can be fused into one gadget with phase $\varphi_1 \pm \varphi_2$. This happens when $P_1 P_2 \ket{G} = \pm \ket{G}$, i.e.\@ $P_1$ and $P_2$ differ only by a stabilizer of the graph state. The resulting entropy is a descendant from the one-gadget case, i.e. $S_0 -1 + h_2(\frac{1}{2}(1-\sin (\varphi_1 \pm \varphi_2)))$ or $S_0 + h_2(\sin^2((\varphi_1 \pm \varphi_2)/2))$; the latter can violate the bound of $S_0 + h_2(\sin^2(\varphi_1/2)) + h_2(\sin^2(\varphi_2/2))$. For a randomly chosen set of Paulis, gadget fusion is exponentially suppressed.

\subsection{General scheme for multiple gadgets}

We now apply $N$ number of gadgets $U_i$ to the graph state $| G \rangle$. The result is a superposition of $2^N$ terms of the form
\begin{equation}
  \underbrace{P_N^{z_N} \ldots P_2^{z_2} P_1^{z_1}}_{Q_{\mathbf{z}}} | G \rangle = \eta_{\mathbf{z}} (I \otimes R_{\mathbf{z}}) | \Phi_D \rangle_{A_e, B_e} \otimes | \mathbf{x} \rangle_{A_l} \otimes | \mathbf{y} \rangle_{B_l},
  \label{eq:superposition}
\end{equation}
where $\eta_{\mathbf{z}} \in \{ 1, i, - 1, - i \}$ is the phase factor of the bitstring transformation associated with a specific choice of $\mathbf{z} = \{ z_1, z_2, \ldots, z_N \}$.

Similarly to the two-gadget case, we create the matrix $M$ of size $2^N\times 2^N$ with submatrices
\begin{equation}
  M_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}} = \sum_{\mathbf{z} \,:\, \mathbf{x}
  (\mathbf{z}) = \mathbf{u}, \mathbf{y} (\mathbf{z}) = \mathbf{v}}
  \beta_{\mathbf{z}} R_{\mathbf{z}} ,
\end{equation}
where again $\beta_{\mathbf{z}} = \eta_{\mathbf{z}}  (- i)^{\sum_i z_i}  \prod_i \cos^{1 - z_i} (\varphi_i/2) \sin^{z_i} (\varphi_i/2)$ is the amplitude of the term corresponding to $\mathbf{z}$ in the superposition.
Matrix $M$ is directly related to the reduced density matrix as $\rho_A = M M^\dagger / D$. The entanglement entropy is given by
\begin{equation}
    S=-\sum_{r=1}^{2^N D} \frac{\sigma_r(M)^2}{D} \log_2 \frac{\sigma_r(M)^2}{D}.
\end{equation}

\textbf{Upper bound.} We can, however, simplify this formula considerably by bounding it from above. Consider dephasing $\rho_A = M M^\dagger / D$ into blocks of constant $\mathbf{u}$,
\begin{equation}
    \Delta_{\mathbf{u}}(\rho_A) = \bigoplus_{\mathbf{u}} \frac{1}{D} \sum_{\mathbf{v}} M_{\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v}} M_{\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v}}^\dagger = \sum_{\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v}} p_{\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v}} \rho_{\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v}},
\end{equation}
where $\Delta_{\mathbf{u}}(\rho_A)$ is the reduced density matrix after dephasing, $p_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}} = \| M_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}} \|^2_F / D$ can be regarded as a mixture probability ($\sum_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}} p_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}}=1$), and $\rho_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}} = \ket{\mathbf{u}} \bra{\mathbf{u}} \otimes M_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}} M_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}}^\dagger / \| M_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}} \|^2_F$ is a normalized density matrix supported in the $\mathbf{u}$-block. Now, using the fact that dephasing cannot decrease entropy~\cite{Nielsen2010}, and using the mixture entropy bound, $S(\sum_i p_i\rho_i) \leq H(\{p_i\}) + \sum_i p_i S(\rho_i)$~\cite{Nielsen2010}, we find that the entropy of the state is bounded as
\begin{equation}
    S \le S(\Delta_{\mathbf{u}}(\rho_A)) \le H(\{p_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}}\}) + \sum_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}} p_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}} S(\rho_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}}),
\end{equation}
where $H(\{x_i\}) = -\sum_i x_i \log_2 x_i$ is Shannon entropy. Finally, we use the fact that $S(\rho_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}})$ can be calculated from the singular values of submatrix $M_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}}$, as $S(\rho_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}}) = H(\{ \sigma_r(M_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}})^2 / \| M_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}} \|^2_F \})$, where now index $r\in\{1,...,D\}$, and that this expression is bounded from above by $\log_2 D = S_0$. This yields the formula
\begin{equation}
    S \le S_0 + H(\{p_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}}\}).
\end{equation}

Let us now assume that either none of the $\mathbf{z}$-branches combine in the superposition in Eq.~\eqref{eq:superposition} (i.e., $Q_{\mathbf{z}} \ket{G} \neq \gamma_{\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{z}'} Q_{\mathbf{z}'} \ket{G}$, where $\gamma_{\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{z}'} \in \{1, i, -1, -i\}$), or they do so not coherently (i.e., $Q_{\mathbf{z}} \ket{G} = \gamma_{\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{z}'} Q_{\mathbf{z}'} \ket{G}$, but $\text{Re}[\beta_{\mathbf{z}}^* \beta_{\mathbf{z}'} \gamma_{\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{z}'}] = 0$). In this case, the block probabilities $p_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}}$ are sums of the independent $\mathbf{z}$-branch distributions $|\beta_{\mathbf{z}}|^2$, so that $H(\{p_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v}}\}) \le H(\{|\beta_{\mathbf{z}}|^2\}) = \sum_i h_2(\sin^2(\varphi_i/2))$. Thus, we have a stricter upper bound on entanglement,
\begin{equation}
    S \le S_0 + \sum_i h_2\!\left(\sin^2 \frac{\varphi_i}{2}\right),
    \label{eq:entropy_bound}
\end{equation}
where the sum is taken over only the gadgets that cross the bipartition. 
Note that in the sum, we can also discard the gadgets that, after transforming using a stabilizer of the state, are supported on one side of the bipartition.

Violation of the bound in Eq.~\eqref{eq:entropy_bound} can only happen if $\mathbf{z}$-branches combine coherently, that is, $Q_{\mathbf{z}} \ket{G} = \gamma_{\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{z}'} Q_{\mathbf{z}'} \ket{G}$ and $\text{Re}[\beta_{\mathbf{z}}^* \beta_{\mathbf{z}'} \gamma_{\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{z}'}] \neq 0$. This means we can find a minimal set $\mathcal{T}$ of gadget indices (ordered according to how gadgets are applied on the state) for which
\begin{align}
    \prod_{i\in\mathcal{T}} P_i &= \pm \text{Stab}(\ket{G}) \quad \text{for even number of gadgets in }\mathcal{T},\\
    \prod_{i\in\mathcal{T}} P_i &= \pm i\, \text{Stab}(\ket{G}) \quad \text{for odd number of gadgets in }\mathcal{T}.
\end{align}
We term this situation ``coherent branch interference''. For example, when $\mathcal{T}$ contains only two gadgets, both in the same commuting region, it corresponds to a gadget fusion scenario in the ZX-calculus. Many violations of the entropy bound in Eq.~\eqref{eq:entropy_bound} can be removed by fusing gadgets that differ by a stabilizer of the state.

\textbf{Lower bound.} Assuming no coherent branch interference, the states $Q_{\mathbf z}\ket{G}$ appearing in Eq.~\eqref{eq:superposition} are distinct and orthogonal, and can be completed to the stabilizer-orbit basis $\{Q\ket{G}\}$, with measurement probabilities $|\beta_{\mathbf z}|^2$ (and zero otherwise). We apply the Maassen-Uffink uncertainty relation~\cite{Maassen1988} between this basis and a complete product basis containing the Schmidt vectors $\ket{a_j}\ket{b_j}$ of the final state, for which the measurement entropy is the entanglement entropy $S$ that we wish to bound. Since the graph state has a flat Schmidt spectrum of rank $D=2^{S_0}$, every $Q\ket{G}=(Q_A\otimes Q_B)\ket{G}$ has the same Schmidt coefficients $1/\sqrt D$ and hence overlaps at most $c=1/\sqrt D$ with any product state. Maassen-Uffink therefore gives $S+H(\{|\beta_{\mathbf z}|^2\}) \geq -2\log_2 c = S_0$. Using $H(\{|\beta_{\mathbf z}|^2\})=\sum_i h_2[\sin^2(\varphi_i/2)]$ yields the lower bound for entanglement entropy,
\begin{equation}
    S \ge S_0 - \sum_i h_2\!\left(\sin^2 \frac{\varphi_i}{2}\right).
    \label{eq:lower_entropy_bound}
\end{equation}

\textbf{Comments.} Note that the bounds in Eq.~\eqref{eq:entropy_bound} and \eqref{eq:lower_entropy_bound} are much stricter than the bound in Eq.~\eqref{eq:generic_entropy_bound}, which can be seen in Fig.~\ref{fig:generic_bound_comparison} and by expanding these expressions for small angles $\varphi_i$, which yields an expression proportional to $\varphi_i^2 \log_2 \varphi_i$ in the leading order,
\begin{equation}
    |S - S_0| \le \frac{1}{4} \sum_i \left( \frac{1}{\ln 2} + 2 - 2 \log_2 \varphi_i \right) \varphi_i^2 + \mathcal{O}(\varphi_i^4 \log_2 \varphi_i).
\end{equation}

The von Neumann entropy bounds can be adapted to R\'enyi entropies $S_\alpha$, where $\alpha$ is the R\'enyi index. The upper bound derivation proceeds similarly, with $h_2(x)$ replaced by the R\'enyi binary entropy $h_2^{\alpha}(x) = (1-\alpha)^{-1} \log_2 (x^\alpha + (1-x)^\alpha)$. The lower bound derivation differs slightly, as the R\'enyi Maassen-Uffink relation pairs entropies with conjugate indices, giving $S_\alpha+H_{\alpha'}(\{|\beta_{\mathbf z}|^2\}) \geq -2\log_2 c$, where $1/\alpha+1/\alpha'=2$. As a consequence, the bounds for R\'enyi entropies are not symmetric,
\begin{equation}
    S_0 - \sum_i h_2^{\alpha'}\!\left(\sin^2 \frac{\varphi_i}{2}\right)
    \le
    S_\alpha
    \le
    S_0 + \sum_i h_2^{\alpha}\!\left(\sin^2 \frac{\varphi_i}{2}\right),
    \qquad \frac{1}{\alpha} + \frac{1}{\alpha'} = 2.
\end{equation}

