\section{Bounds on magic (log stabilizer extent)}

Let us define the log stabilizer extent~\cite{Bravyi2019, Heimendahl2021, Seddon2021} as
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{M}(\ket{\psi}) = \log_2 \xi(\ket{\psi}),
\end{equation}
where $\xi(\ket{\psi})$ is a stabilizer extent. $\mathcal{M}$ is a logarithmic version of the magic/non-stabilizerness monotone $\xi$ (analogous to the logarithmic negativity or log robustness). The stabilizer extent is defined as
\begin{equation}
    \xi(\ket{\psi}) = \min_{\ket{\psi}=\sum_i \alpha_i \ket{S_i}} (\sum_i |\alpha_i|)^2,
\end{equation}
where the minimum is taken over all possible stabilizer decompositions of the state, $\ket{S_i}$ are the stabilizer states in the decomposition, and $\alpha_i$ are their coefficients.

We aim to bound $\mathcal M$ additively from above. For this purpose, let us first find the maximal increase in $\mathcal M$ after application of one Pauli gadget on an arbitrary state. Take an optimal decomposition of the original state
\begin{equation}
\ket{\psi} = \sum_j \alpha_j\ket{S_j}, \qquad \sum_j|\alpha_j|=\sqrt{\xi_0}.
\end{equation}
We now examine $e^{i\varphi P/2}\ket{S_j}$ for each stabilizer state.
After absorbing signs, global phases, and possibly multiplying by the Clifford $P$, we may write
\begin{equation}
  e^{i\varphi P/2} \ket{S_j} = M \ket{S_j} + i m P \ket{S_j},
\end{equation}
where $M = \max\{|\cos(\varphi/2)|, |\sin(\varphi/2)|\}, m = \min\{|\cos(\varphi/2)|, |\sin(\varphi/2)|\}$.
We also notice that the state $\ket{T_j} = (\ket{S_j}+i P\ket{S_j})/\sqrt{2}$ is a stabilizer state. Therefore, we can decompose
\begin{equation}
  M \ket{S_j}+i m P \ket{S_j} = (M-m) \ket{S_j} + \sqrt{2} m \ket{T_j}.
\end{equation}
The stabilizer extent, assuming this is the optimal decomposition for each term, is
\begin{equation}
  \xi = \xi_0 \left(M + (\sqrt{2} - 1) m\right)^2,
\end{equation}
which is the upper bound for the change in stabilizer extent in general.

This generalizes readily to the graph-state-and-Pauli-gadgets normal form, where the upper bound for the log stabilizer extent is a sum of contributions from gadgets $\mathcal{O}$ whose supports are not stabilizers of the state,
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{M}(\ket{\psi})
  \le 2\sum_{j \in \mathcal{O}}
  \log_2 \! \left(  \max(|\sin\frac{\varphi_j}{2}|,|\cos\frac{\varphi_j}{2}|) \right. 
  \left. + (\sqrt2-1) \min(|\sin\frac{\varphi_j}{2}|,|\cos\frac{\varphi_j}{2}|)  \right).
\end{equation}
The expression, after a bit of algebra, simplifies to
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{M}(\ket{\psi}) \le 2 \sum_{j \in \mathcal{O}}
    \log_2\! \left[ \sqrt{1-|\sin \varphi_j|} + \sqrt{1-|\cos \varphi_j|} \right]\!.
\end{equation}

