\section{Analytical Foundations of Dual-Decoder Error Cancellation}
\label{sec:supp_dual_decoder_protocol}
\label{sec:appendix_derivations}

In this section, we establish the analytical foundations of the dual-decoder virtual error cancellation architecture.
Fault-tolerant quantum computing architectures require low-latency real-time decoding to execute feedforward operations within coherence deadlines, typically employing fast minimum-weight perfect matching (MWPM) or union-find algorithms.
Asynchronous offline processing with more accurate tensor-network, belief-propagation, or neural-network decoders provides a rich stream of correlation information without delaying execution.
For an $n$-qubit logical block with Pauli group $\mathcal{P}_n$, comparing the real-time correction $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{rt}}(\bm{s}) \in \mathcal{P}_n$ against an offline reference correction $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{off}}(\bm{s}) \in \mathcal{P}_n$ partitions the syndrome space $\Omega$ into $K = 4^n$ disjoint sectors,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}_Q \equiv \big\{\bm{s} \in \Omega :\; \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{rt}}(\bm{s})\cdot \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{off}}(\bm{s}) \sim Q\big\}, \qquad Q \in \mathcal{P}_n,
\label{eq:app_dd_sector_definition}
\end{equation}
where $\sim$ denotes equality up to an overall phase factor.
Agreement between the decoders defines the consensus sector $\mathcal{S}_I$, which captures the vast majority of syndrome measurements with dominant probability share $\pi_I = 1 - \mathcal{O}(p_L)$.
Conversely, disagreement along logical Pauli direction $Q \in \mathcal{P}_n \setminus \{I\}$ partitions the remaining shots into $4^n - 1$ contested directional sectors $\mathcal{S}_Q$, each characterized by an elevated conditional logical error centroid $\bm{p}_Q \equiv \EE_{\bm{s}}[\bm{p}(\bm{s}) \mid \bm{s} \in \mathcal{S}_Q]$.
Supplementary Fig.~\ref{fig:supp_dual_decoder_cartoon} illustrates this partition and the resulting reweighting in its simplest form, with all contested sectors merged into a single disagreement sector.

The mathematical analysis proceeds across three subsections:
In Subsection~\ref{subsec:app_proof_pauli_4sector_invertibility}, we establish affine independence and L\'evy--Desplanques invertibility for the minimal four-sector Pauli noise model ($n = 1$ and $M = 3$), covering Hypotheses~H1--H3, simplex geometry, the parameterized CSS model, the L\'evy--Desplanques theorem and Varah bound (Lemma~\ref{lem:app_levy_desplanques}), Theorem~\ref{thm:app_levy_desplanques_pauli}, congruent covariance factorization (Proposition~\ref{thm:app_covariance_factorization}) with Sherman--Morrison inversion (Lemma~\ref{lem:app_sherman_morrison_categorical}), variational collapse to closed-form sector weights (Proposition~\ref{thm:app_variational_collapse}), fault-tolerant overhead scaling in matrix quadratic form (Theorem~\ref{thm:app_asymptotic_overhead}), and uncoupled check basis reduction (Corollary~\ref{cor:app_overhead_uncoupled}).
In Subsection~\ref{subsec:app_proof_distance_suppression}, we analyze residual logical error under calibration perturbations and establish distance suppression, covering perturbed parameters $\hat{\pi}_k, \hat{\bm{p}}_k$, trace-preservation normalization $\bar{w}$ and the residual error vector $\bm{p}_{\mathrm{res}}$, the exact first-order differential and binary reduction (Proposition~\ref{thm:app_exact_differential}), uniform shift invariance (Lemma~\ref{lem:app_uniform_shift_invariance}), Theorem~\ref{thm:app_distance_suppression}, and physical comparisons with postselection and physical probabilistic error cancellation.
Finally, in Subsection~\ref{subsec:app_decoder_asymmetry_derivation}, we provide the microscopic derivation of the dual-decoder error asymmetry ratio and conditional centroids, covering joint error indicators $E_{\mathrm{rt},j}, E_{\mathrm{off},j}$, asymmetry ratio $r_j = (p_{L,j}' - p_{I,j})/(p_{L,j} - p_{I,j})$, contested failure probability $p_{X,j} = 1/(1+r_j)$, overhead slope $\eta_j^{\mathrm{dd}} = (1+r_j)/(1-\alpha_j)$ [Eq.~\eqref{eq:dd_overhead_eta_formula}], and interpolation between ideal oracle and symmetric tie-break limits.

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Affine Independence and L\'evy--Desplanques Invertibility for the 4-Sector Pauli Model}{Affine Independence and Levy-Desplanques Invertibility for the 4-Sector Pauli Model}}
\label{subsec:app_proof_pauli_4sector_invertibility}

We now provide the complete proof suite for the minimal four-sector logical Pauli mitigation architecture.
Consider arbitrary single-qubit logical Pauli noise ($M = 3$, with alphabet $\mathcal{Q} \equiv \{Q_1, Q_2, Q_3\} = \{X, Z, Y\}$).
By Proposition~\ref{thm:minimal_resolving_basis}, resolving $M = 3$ independent error processes requires $K = M + 1 = 4$ syndrome sectors:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Reference sector $\mathcal{S}_0$ ($k = 0$):} Measurement shots assigned to the consensus sector ($\chi_X(\bm{s}) = \chi_Z(\bm{s}) = 0$, giving $\chi(\bm{s}) = 0$), where residual logical errors are suppressed by consensus decoding to $\bm{p}_0 = \alpha \bar{\bm{p}} = \mathcal{O}(p_L) = \mathcal{O}((p/p_{\mathrm{th}})^{(d+1)/2})$, with agreement error suppression ratio $\alpha \equiv \|\bm{p}_0\| / \|\bar{\bm{p}}\| \in (0, 1)$ and dominant occurrence probability $\pi_0 = 1 - \sum_{k=1}^3 \pi_k = 1 - \mathcal{O}((p/p_{\mathrm{th}})^{(d+1)/2})$.
\item \emph{Directional sectors $\mathcal{S}_1, \mathcal{S}_2, \mathcal{S}_3$ ($k \in \{1, 2, 3\}$):} Measurement shots classified by the directional syndrome partitioning map $\chi(\bm{s}) \equiv \chi_X(\bm{s}) + 2\chi_Z(\bm{s})$, isolating directional $X$ faults ($\mathcal{S}_1$, with $\chi=1$), directional $Z$ faults ($\mathcal{S}_2$, with $\chi=2$), and joint faults ($\mathcal{S}_3$, with $\chi=3$, isolating $Y = XZ$).
Directional identification isolates fault configurations producing conditional failure probabilities $p_{1,1} = p_{c,X} - \mathcal{O}(p_L)$ and $p_{2,2} = p_{c,Z} - \mathcal{O}(p_L)$ with classifier confidences $p_{c,X}, p_{c,Z} \in (0, 1]$, and joint centroid $\bm{p}_3$ governed by physical cross-basis correlation $\rho \in [0, 1]$.
The directional sectors occur with probabilities $\pi_1 = \Theta(p_{L,X})$ and $\pi_2 = \Theta(p_{L,Z})$, each of order $(p/p_{\mathrm{th}})^{(d+1)/2}$, and $\pi_3 = \rho_L p_L + (1-\rho_L)p_L^2 = \mathcal{O}(p_L)$.
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Foundational Sector Hypotheses for the Four-Sector Protocol}
\label{subsubsec:app_foundational_hypotheses}

The exact algebraic channel inversion, variance scaling, and noise suppression of the four-sector protocol hold generally for any discrete syndrome partition satisfying three foundational sector hypotheses:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\emph{H1.}] \emph{Non-degenerate resolving basis and quantitative conditioning:} The smallest singular value of the coordinate displacement matrix $\bm{V} \in \RR^{3 \times 3}$ is bounded below by a code-distance-independent positive constant, $\sigma_{\min}(\bm{V}) \ge v_{\min} > 0$.
This condition guarantees full algebraic rank $\operatorname{rank}(\bm{V}) = 3$, non-zero simplex volume $\operatorname{Vol}(\Delta) \ge v_{\min}^3 / 6 > 0$, and stable channel inversion.
\item[\emph{H2.}] \emph{Per-channel sampling adequacy:} Each directional sector probability is positive and satisfies $0 < \pi_k = \mathcal{O}(p_L)$, and the reference-sector leakage along that channel is suppressed relative to that sector probability,
\begin{equation}
u_{0,k} \equiv (\bm{V}^{-1}\bm{p}_0)_k = \mathcal{O}(\alpha \pi_k),
\label{eq:app_H2_per_channel}
\end{equation}
which implies $\pi_k \gg [(\bm{V}^{-1}\bm{p}_0)_k]^2$ and makes the directional variance penalty $\mathcal{O}(\pi_k)$ rather than $\mathcal{O}(u_{0,k}^2/\pi_k)$.
\item[\emph{H3.}] \emph{Dominant reference sector and error suppression:} The reference sector occurs with dominant probability $\pi_0 = 1 - \sum_{k \ge 1} \pi_k = 1 - \mathcal{O}((p/p_{\mathrm{th}})^{(d+1)/2})$ and suppressed conditional error rate $\|\bm{p}_0\| = \alpha \|\bar{\bm{p}}\| = \mathcal{O}((p/p_{\mathrm{th}})^{(d+1)/2})$, where $\alpha \equiv \|\bm{p}_0\| / \|\bar{\bm{p}}\| \in (0, 1)$ is the agreement error suppression ratio.
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Simplex Geometry and Differential Sensitivity}
\label{subsubsec:app_simplex_geometry}

Geometrically, the four conditional error centroid vectors $\{\bm{p}_0, \bm{p}_1, \bm{p}_2, \bm{p}_3\}$ form the vertices of a 3-simplex $\Delta \subset [0, 1]^3$ in the three-dimensional logical Pauli error space.
The coordinate displacement matrix $\bm{V} \equiv (\bm{v}_1, \bm{v}_2, \bm{v}_3) = (\bm{p}_1 - \bm{p}_0, \bm{p}_2 - \bm{p}_0, \bm{p}_3 - \bm{p}_0) \in \RR^{3 \times 3}$ is given explicitly by
\begin{equation}
\bm{V} = \begin{pmatrix} p_{1,1} - p_{1,0} & p_{1,2} - p_{1,0} & p_{1,3} - p_{1,0} \\ p_{2,1} - p_{2,0} & p_{2,2} - p_{2,0} & p_{2,3} - p_{2,0} \\ p_{3,1} - p_{3,0} & p_{3,2} - p_{3,0} & p_{3,3} - p_{3,0} \end{pmatrix}.
\label{eq:app_displacement_matrix_V}
\end{equation}
The columns of $\bm{V}$ form the edge basis of this simplex with origin anchored at the reference vertex $\bm{p}_0$, with Euclidean volume $\operatorname{Vol}(\Delta) = \frac{1}{6} |\det(\bm{V})|$.
By Proposition~\ref{thm:minimal_resolving_basis}, non-singularity $\det(\bm{V}) \ne 0$ certifies that the four centroids are affinely independent and that discrete channel unbiasing expresses the ideal target $\bm{0}$ in exterior barycentric coordinates with $w_0^* > 1$ and $w_k^* < 0$.
Physically, $\bm{V}$ represents the differential sensitivity matrix of the syndrome coarse-graining: each diagonal entry $V_{k,k} \equiv p_{k,k} - p_{k,0}$ measures primary directional contrast along Pauli axis $k$, while each off-diagonal entry $V_{i,k} \equiv p_{i,k} - p_{i,0}$ ($i \ne k$) measures cross-talk and channel leakage between non-commuting Pauli error mechanisms.
Channel unbiasing is well-conditioned whenever directional contrast strictly dominates channel cross-talk.

\subsubsection{Concrete Parameterized Model: Symmetric CSS Codes under Correlated Pauli Noise}
\label{subsubsec:app_concrete_parameterized_model}

To illustrate the four-sector framework concretely, consider a symmetric CSS surface code subject to phenomenological or circuit-level Pauli noise parameterized by single-axis error rates and cross-basis correlation $\rho \in [0, 1]$.
Let directional sectors herald elevated single-axis failure probabilities $p_{c,X}, p_{c,Z} \in (0, 1]$ along $X$ and $Z$, respectively, with residual reference errors suppressed as $\epsilon_Q = \alpha_Q \bar{p}_{L,Q} = \Theta((p/p_{\mathrm{th}})^{(d+1)/2})$.

We derive the microscopic structure of the joint directional sector $\mathcal{S}_3$ ($\chi_X = \chi_Z = 1$) from the physical composition of logical errors.
Syndromes assigned to $\mathcal{S}_3$ herald the simultaneous presence of elevated failure mechanisms across both check bases, decomposing into two disjoint microscopic branches:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Correlated $Y$ faults (fraction $\rho \in [0, 1]$):} A cross-basis correlated error mechanism (such as circuit-level $Y$ faults or simultaneous $X$ and $Z$ errors from two-qubit gate cross-talk) occurs with fraction $\rho$, directly generating a logical $Y$ error with conditional probability $p_{c,Y} \in (0, 1]$.
\item \emph{Independent joint faults (fraction $1 - \rho$):} Independent fault mechanisms occur across the $X$ and $Z$ decoding graphs.
Under the product distribution, conditional outcomes evaluate to: (i) logical $X$ only with probability $p_{c,X}(1 - p_{c,Z})$; (ii) logical $Z$ only with probability $(1 - p_{c,X})p_{c,Z}$; and (iii) joint logical $X$ and $Z$ errors with probability $p_{c,X} p_{c,Z}$, which manifest in a CSS code as logical $Y_L = X_L Z_L$.
\end{enumerate}
Combining both branches, the conditional error rates of sector $\mathcal{S}_3$ along the three logical Pauli axes evaluate to $p_{1,3} = (1 - \rho) p_{c,X}(1 - p_{c,Z}) + \epsilon_X''$, $p_{2,3} = (1 - \rho)(1 - p_{c,X}) p_{c,Z} + \epsilon_Z''$, and $p_{3,3} = (1 - \rho) p_{c,X} p_{c,Z} + \rho p_{c,Y} + \epsilon_Y'''$, where $\epsilon_X'', \epsilon_Z'', \epsilon_Y''' = \mathcal{O}(p_L)$ denote higher-weight background corrections.
Subtracting the reference centroid $\bm{p}_0 = \mathcal{O}(p_L)$ yields the coordinate displacement matrix $\bm{V} = \bm{V}_{\mathrm{LO}} + \Delta\bm{V}$, where the leading-order displacement matrix is
\begin{equation}
\bm{V}_{\mathrm{LO}} \equiv \begin{pmatrix} p_{c,X} & 0 & (1 - \rho) p_{c,X}(1 - p_{c,Z}) \\ 0 & p_{c,Z} & (1 - \rho)(1 - p_{c,X}) p_{c,Z} \\ 0 & 0 & (1 - \rho) p_{c,X} p_{c,Z} + \rho p_{c,Y} \end{pmatrix},
\label{eq:app_V_LO_def}
\end{equation}
and $\Delta\bm{V} = \mathcal{O}(p_L)$.
Because $\bm{V}_{\mathrm{LO}}$ is upper triangular, its determinant is given directly by the product of its diagonal elements,
\begin{equation}
\det(\bm{V}_{\mathrm{LO}}) = p_{c,X} p_{c,Z} \big[ (1 - \rho) p_{c,X} p_{c,Z} + \rho p_{c,Y} \big].
\label{eq:app_det_V_LO}
\end{equation}
For all physical classifier confidences $p_{c,X}, p_{c,Z}, p_{c,Y} > 0$ and all correlation fractions $\rho \in [0, 1]$, the diagonal entries are strictly positive, guaranteeing $\det(\bm{V}_{\mathrm{LO}}) > 0$.
At the canonical graph-decoder tie-break baseline ($p_{c,X} = p_{c,Z} = p_{c,Y} = 1/2$), the entries evaluate to $V_{13} = V_{23} = (1-\rho)/4$ and $V_{33} = (1+\rho)/4$, where the denominator $4$ arises from $(p_c)^2 = (1/2)^2 = 1/4$.
Conversely, in the idealized oracle limit where classifier confidences approach certainty ($p_{c,Q} \to 1$), the off-diagonal entries vanish identically and $\lim_{p_{c,Q} \to 1} \bm{V}_{\mathrm{LO}} = \bm{I}_{3 \times 3}$ for all $\rho \in [0, 1]$.

Two distinct small parameters control the structure of $\bm{V}$.
The perturbation $\Delta\bm{V} = \bm{V} - \bm{V}_{\mathrm{LO}} = \mathcal{O}(p_L)$ measures departure from the leading-order form, whereas channel decoupling is governed by the off-diagonal component
\begin{equation}
\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}} \equiv \bm{V} - \operatorname{diag}(V_{1,1}, V_{2,2}, V_{3,3}).
\label{eq:app_delta_V_off_def}
\end{equation}
The two parameters satisfy the norm bound
\begin{equation}
\|\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}}\|_\infty \le \|\Delta\bm{V}\|_\infty + (1 - \rho) \max_{Q} p_{c,Q},
\label{eq:app_delta_V_off_bound}
\end{equation}
confirming that off-diagonal channel leakage is suppressed when either $\rho \to 1$ (correlated double errors) or $p_{c,Q} \to 1$ (oracle-quality classification).

\subsubsection{Affine Independence, L\'evy--Desplanques Diagonal Dominance, and Condition Number Bounds}
\label{subsubsec:app_levy_desplanques}
\label{subsec:app_levy_desplanques_stability}

By the L\'evy--Desplanques theorem and Varah's inverse norm bound, strict row-wise diagonal dominance of the displacement matrix $\bm{V}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_displacement_matrix_V} guarantees both non-singularity of the system and $\mathcal{O}(1)$ spectral conditioning.

\begin{lemma}[L\'evy--Desplanques Theorem and Varah Bound]
\label{lem:app_levy_desplanques}
\label{lem:levy_desplanques_dominance}
Let $\bm{M} = (M_{ij}) \in \RR^{n \times n}$ be a strictly diagonally dominant matrix whose row margins satisfy
\begin{equation}
d_i \equiv |M_{ii}| - \sum_{j \neq i} |M_{ij}| > 0 \qquad \text{for all } i \in \{1, \dots, n\}.
\label{eq:app_levy_desplanques_condition}
\end{equation}
Then $\bm{M}$ is non-singular ($\det(\bm{M}) \neq 0$), and its inverse satisfies Varah's infinity-norm bound
\begin{equation}
\|\bm{M}^{-1}\|_\infty \le \frac{1}{\min_{1 \le i \le n} d_i}.
\label{eq:app_varah_bound}
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For any non-zero vector $\bm{x} \in \RR^n \setminus \{\bm{0}\}$, choose an index $k \in \{1, \dots, n\}$ attaining the maximum component magnitude $|x_k| = \|\bm{x}\|_\infty > 0$.
By the reverse triangle inequality on row $k$ of $\bm{M}\bm{x}$,
\begin{equation}
\|\bm{M}\bm{x}\|_\infty \ge |(\bm{M}\bm{x})_k| = \left| M_{kk} x_k + \sum_{j \neq k} M_{kj} x_j \right| \ge |M_{kk}| |x_k| - \sum_{j \neq k} |M_{kj}| |x_j| \ge d_k \|\bm{x}\|_\infty \ge \left(\min_{1 \le i \le n} d_i\right) \|\bm{x}\|_\infty > 0.
\end{equation}
Hence $\bm{M}\bm{x} \neq \bm{0}$ for all $\bm{x} \neq \bm{0}$, certifying $\det(\bm{M}) \neq 0$.
Substituting $\bm{x} = \bm{M}^{-1}\bm{y}$ proves Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_varah_bound}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[L\'evy--Desplanques Invertibility and Closed-Form Pauli Sector Weights]
\label{thm:app_levy_desplanques_pauli}
\label{thm:levy_desplanques_margin}
For all physical classifier confidences $p_{c,X}, p_{c,Z}, p_{c,Y} \in (0, 1]$ and cross-basis correlation parameters $\rho \in [0, 1]$, the leading-order coordinate displacement matrix $\bm{V}_{\mathrm{LO}}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_V_LO_def} satisfies universal strict diagonal dominance with positive row margins
\begin{equation}
|V_{ii}| - \sum_{j \neq i} |V_{ij}| \ge p_{c,X} p_{c,Z} > 0 \qquad \text{for all } i \in \{1, 2, 3\}.
\label{eq:app_strict_diagonal_dominance}
\end{equation}
Consequently, below the fault-tolerant threshold ($p < p_{\mathrm{th}}$), the physical displacement matrix $\bm{V}$ is non-singular ($\det(\bm{V}) \neq 0$) with bounded spectral condition number $\sigma_{\min}(\bm{V}) \ge v_{\min} > 0$ and $\kappa(\bm{V}) \equiv \|\bm{V}\|_2 \|\bm{V}^{-1}\|_2 = \kappa(\bm{V}_{\mathrm{LO}}) + \mathcal{O}(p_L) = \mathcal{O}(1)$ (approaching $1 + \mathcal{O}(p_L)$ in the ideal oracle limit $p_{c,Q} \to 1$), and the four sector centroids $\{\bm{p}_0, \bm{p}_1, \bm{p}_2, \bm{p}_3\}$ are affinely independent in $\RR^3$, spanning a non-degenerate $3$-simplex.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
We evaluate the diagonal dominance margin $|V_{ii}| - \sum_{j \neq i} |V_{ij}|$ for each row of $\bm{V}_{\mathrm{LO}}$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Row 1 ($X$-axis):} The diagonal entry is $V_{11} = p_{c,X}$, and the only non-zero off-diagonal entry is $V_{13} = (1-\rho) p_{c,X}(1 - p_{c,Z}) \ge 0$.
The row margin evaluates to
\begin{equation}
|V_{11}| - \sum_{j \neq 1} |V_{1j}| = p_{c,X} - (1 - \rho) p_{c,X}(1 - p_{c,Z}) = p_{c,X} \big[ p_{c,Z} + \rho(1 - p_{c,Z}) \big] \ge p_{c,X} p_{c,Z} > 0.
\end{equation}
\item \emph{Row 2 ($Z$-axis):} The diagonal entry is $V_{22} = p_{c,Z}$, and the only non-zero off-diagonal entry is $V_{23} = (1-\rho)(1 - p_{c,X}) p_{c,Z} \ge 0$.
By exact symmetry under exchanging $X$ and $Z$, the row margin evaluates to
\begin{equation}
|V_{22}| - \sum_{j \neq 2} |V_{2j}| = p_{c,Z} \big[ p_{c,X} + \rho(1 - p_{c,X}) \big] \ge p_{c,X} p_{c,Z} > 0.
\end{equation}
\item \emph{Row 3 ($Y$-axis):} The diagonal entry is $V_{33} = (1-\rho) p_{c,X} p_{c,Z} + \rho p_{c,Y}$, and the off-diagonal entries vanish identically ($V_{31} = V_{32} = 0$).
For physical error mechanisms with $p_{c,Y} \ge p_{c,X} p_{c,Z}$ (and for any $p_{c,Y} > 0$ when $\rho > 0$), the margin satisfies
\begin{equation}
|V_{33}| - \sum_{j \neq 3} |V_{3j}| = (1 - \rho) p_{c,X} p_{c,Z} + \rho p_{c,Y} \ge \min\{p_{c,X} p_{c,Z}, p_{c,Y}\} > 0.
\end{equation}
In particular, when $p_{c,X} = p_{c,Z} = p_{c,Y} = p_c$, the third row margin is $(1-\rho)p_c^2 + \rho p_c \ge p_c^2 \ge p_{c,X} p_{c,Z} > 0$.
\end{enumerate}
Therefore, universal strict diagonal dominance $|V_{ii}| - \sum_{j \neq i} |V_{ij}| \ge p_{c,X} p_{c,Z} > 0$ holds across all rows for all $p_{c,Q} \in (0, 1]$ and all $\rho \in [0, 1]$.
At the MWPM tie-break baseline ($p_{c,Q} = 1/2$), the margin is at least $(1/2)(1/2) = 1/4 > 0$.
In the oracle limit ($p_{c,Q} \to 1$), the margin reaches $1$.

Below the fault-tolerant threshold ($p < p_{\mathrm{th}}$), the physical perturbation satisfies $\|\Delta\bm{V}\|_\infty = \mathcal{O}(p_L) \ll p_{c,X} p_{c,Z}$, so that the perturbed physical matrix $\bm{V} = \bm{V}_{\mathrm{LO}} + \Delta\bm{V}$ remains strictly diagonally dominant with margin at least $p_{c,X} p_{c,Z} - \mathcal{O}(p_L) > 0$.
By Lemma~\ref{lem:app_levy_desplanques}, every strictly diagonally dominant matrix is non-singular, certifying $\det(\bm{V}) \neq 0$.
Direct evaluation confirms that the perturbed determinant satisfies
\begin{equation}
\det(\bm{V}) = \det(\bm{V}_{\mathrm{LO}}) + \mathcal{O}\big(\|\Delta\bm{V}\|_\infty\big) = p_{c,X} p_{c,Z} \big[ (1 - \rho) p_{c,X} p_{c,Z} + \rho p_{c,Y} \big] + \mathcal{O}(p_L) > 0.
\label{eq:app_det_V_bound}
\end{equation}
By Proposition~\ref{thm:minimal_resolving_basis} (Item 4), the geometric and system matrices satisfy the determinant identities $\det(\bm{A}_{\mathrm{geom}}) = \det(\bm{V}) \ne 0$ and $\det(\bm{A}_{\mathrm{sys}}) = (\prod_{k=0}^3 \pi_k)\det(\bm{V}) \ne 0$, certifying that the four centroids $\{\bm{p}_0, \bm{p}_1, \bm{p}_2, \bm{p}_3\}$ are affinely independent in $\RR^3$ and that the channel-inversion system $\bm{A}_{\mathrm{sys}}\bm{w} = \bm{e}_1$ admits a unique solution.
\end{proof}

\subsubsection{Between-Sector Covariance Factorization and Closed-Form Sector Weights}
\label{subsubsec:app_covariance_factorization_weights}

Let $\bar{\bm{p}} \equiv \sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k \bm{p}_k$ denote the unconditional centroid.
Using $\sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k = 1$, the consensus displacement satisfies
\begin{equation}
\bm{p}_0 - \bar{\bm{p}} = \bm{p}_0 - \sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k \bm{p}_k = (1 - \pi_0)\bm{p}_0 - \sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k \bm{p}_k = -\sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k (\bm{p}_k - \bm{p}_0) = -\bm{V} \bm{\pi}_{1:M},
\label{eq:app_p0_minus_pbar}
\end{equation}
where $\bm{\pi}_{1:M} \equiv (\pi_1, \dots, \pi_M)^\top \in \RR^M$.
For each directional sector $k \in \{1, \dots, M\}$, the centroid displacement evaluates to
\begin{equation}
\bm{p}_k - \bar{\bm{p}} = (\bm{p}_k - \bm{p}_0) + (\bm{p}_0 - \bar{\bm{p}}) = \bm{v}_k - \bm{V}\bm{\pi}_{1:M} = \bm{V}(\bm{e}_k - \bm{\pi}_{1:M}),
\label{eq:app_pk_minus_pbar}
\end{equation}
where $\bm{e}_k$ is the $k$-th standard basis vector in $\RR^M$.

\begin{proposition}[Between-Sector Covariance Factorization]
\label{thm:app_covariance_factorization}
When $K = M+1$, the between-sector covariance matrix $\bm{\Sigma}_S \equiv \sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k (\bm{p}_k - \bar{\bm{p}})(\bm{p}_k - \bar{\bm{p}})^\top \in \RR^{M \times M}$ admits an exact congruent factorization,
\begin{equation}
\bm{\Sigma}_S = \bm{V} \bm{\Sigma}_\pi \bm{V}^\top,
\label{eq:app_sigma_S_factorization}
\end{equation}
where $\bm{\Sigma}_\pi \in \RR^{M \times M}$ is the categorical covariance matrix of the directional sector indicators,
\begin{equation}
\bm{\Sigma}_\pi \equiv \pi_0 \bm{\pi}_{1:M}\bm{\pi}_{1:M}^\top + \sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k (\bm{e}_k - \bm{\pi}_{1:M})(\bm{e}_k - \bm{\pi}_{1:M})^\top.
\end{equation}
Furthermore, the inverse between-sector covariance matrix evaluates to
\begin{equation}
\bm{\Sigma}_S^{-1} = \bm{V}^{-\top} \bm{\Sigma}_\pi^{-1} \bm{V}^{-1} = \bm{V}^{-\top} \left( \bm{D}_\pi^{-1} + \frac{\bm{1}_M \bm{1}_M^\top}{\pi_0} \right) \bm{V}^{-1},
\label{eq:app_sigma_S_inverse_exact}
\end{equation}
where $\bm{D}_\pi \equiv \operatorname{diag}(\pi_1, \dots, \pi_M)$ and $\bm{1}_M \equiv (1, \dots, 1)^\top \in \RR^M$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Substituting Eqs.~\eqref{eq:app_p0_minus_pbar} and~\eqref{eq:app_pk_minus_pbar} into the between-sector covariance definition yields
\begin{align}
\bm{\Sigma}_S &\equiv \sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k (\bm{p}_k - \bar{\bm{p}})(\bm{p}_k - \bar{\bm{p}})^\top \nonumber \\
&= \pi_0 \big(-\bm{V}\bm{\pi}_{1:M}\big)\big(-\bm{V}\bm{\pi}_{1:M}\big)^\top + \sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k \big[\bm{V}(\bm{e}_k - \bm{\pi}_{1:M})\big]\big[\bm{V}(\bm{e}_k - \bm{\pi}_{1:M})\big]^\top \nonumber \\
&= \bm{V} \left[ \pi_0 \bm{\pi}_{1:M}\bm{\pi}_{1:M}^\top + \sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k (\bm{e}_k - \bm{\pi}_{1:M})(\bm{e}_k - \bm{\pi}_{1:M})^\top \right] \bm{V}^\top = \bm{V} \bm{\Sigma}_\pi \bm{V}^\top.
\end{align}
Since $\bm{V}$ is non-singular by Theorem~\ref{thm:app_levy_desplanques_pauli} and $\bm{\Sigma}_\pi$ is invertible by Lemma~\ref{lem:app_sherman_morrison_categorical}, inverting both sides gives $\bm{\Sigma}_S^{-1} = \bm{V}^{-\top} \bm{\Sigma}_\pi^{-1} \bm{V}^{-1}$.
Substituting Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_sigma_q_inverse} completes the proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Sherman--Morrison Inversion of the Categorical Covariance Matrix]
\label{lem:app_sherman_morrison_categorical}
If $K = M+1$, the categorical covariance matrix simplifies to the canonical diagonal-minus-rank-one form,
\begin{equation}
\bm{\Sigma}_\pi = \bm{D}_\pi - \bm{\pi}_{1:M}\bm{\pi}_{1:M}^\top,
\label{eq:app_sigma_q_canonical}
\end{equation}
where $\bm{D}_\pi \equiv \operatorname{diag}(\pi_1, \dots, \pi_M)$, and its exact inverse is given by
\begin{equation}
\bm{\Sigma}_\pi^{-1} = \bm{D}_\pi^{-1} + \frac{\bm{1}_M \bm{1}_M^\top}{\pi_0}.
\label{eq:app_sigma_q_inverse}
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Expanding the sum over $k \in \{1, \dots, M\}$ gives
\begin{align}
\sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k (\bm{e}_k - \bm{\pi}_{1:M})(\bm{e}_k - \bm{\pi}_{1:M})^\top &= \sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k \bm{e}_k \bm{e}_k^\top - \Big(\sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k \bm{e}_k\Big)\bm{\pi}_{1:M}^\top - \bm{\pi}_{1:M}\Big(\sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k \bm{e}_k\Big)^\top + \Big(\sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k\Big)\bm{\pi}_{1:M}\bm{\pi}_{1:M}^\top \nonumber \\
&= \operatorname{diag}(\bm{\pi}_{1:M}) - (1 + \pi_0)\bm{\pi}_{1:M}\bm{\pi}_{1:M}^\top.
\end{align}
Adding $\pi_0 \bm{\pi}_{1:M}\bm{\pi}_{1:M}^\top$, the categorical covariance simplifies to Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_sigma_q_canonical}.
Applying the Sherman--Morrison rank-1 matrix inversion formula,
\begin{equation}
\big(\bm{D}_\pi - \bm{\pi}_{1:M}\bm{\pi}_{1:M}^\top\big)^{-1} = \bm{D}_\pi^{-1} + \frac{\bm{D}_\pi^{-1}\bm{\pi}_{1:M}\bm{\pi}_{1:M}^\top \bm{D}_\pi^{-1}}{1 - \bm{\pi}_{1:M}^\top \bm{D}_\pi^{-1}\bm{\pi}_{1:M}}.
\end{equation}
Observing that $\bm{D}_\pi^{-1}\bm{\pi}_{1:M} = \bm{1}_M \equiv (1, \dots, 1)^\top \in \RR^M$ and $1 - \bm{\pi}_{1:M}^\top \bm{D}_\pi^{-1}\bm{\pi}_{1:M} = 1 - \sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k = \pi_0$, the inverse categorical covariance evaluates exactly to Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_sigma_q_inverse}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Variational Collapse to Closed-Form Sector Weights and Overhead]
\label{thm:app_variational_collapse}
For $K = M+1$ syndrome sectors, the variational filter weights $w_k^* = 1 - \bar{\bm{p}}^\top \bm{\Sigma}_S^{-1} (\bm{p}_k - \bar{\bm{p}})$ collapse identically to the unique algebraic solution of $\bm{A}_{\mathrm{sys}}\bm{w} = \bm{e}_1$,
\begin{equation}
w_0^* = \frac{1 + \bm{1}_M^\top \bm{V}^{-1}\bm{p}_0}{\pi_0}, \qquad w_k^* = -\frac{(\bm{V}^{-1}\bm{p}_0)_k}{\pi_k} \qquad (k \in \{1, \dots, M\}),
\label{eq:app_weights_closed_form}
\end{equation}
and the minimal sampling overhead evaluates to the exact rational expression
\begin{equation}
C_S^* = \frac{\big(1 + \bm{1}_M^\top \bm{V}^{-1}\bm{p}_0\big)^2}{\pi_0} + \sum_{k=1}^M \frac{\big[(\bm{V}^{-1}\bm{p}_0)_k\big]^2}{\pi_k}.
\label{eq:app_overhead_exact}
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:discrete_sector_bound}, the optimal discrete sector filter is governed by the variational expression
\begin{equation}
w_k^* = 1 - \bar{\bm{p}}^\top \bm{\Sigma}_S^{-1} (\bm{p}_k - \bar{\bm{p}}).
\label{eq:app_variational_weights_start}
\end{equation}
Because $\bar{\bm{p}} = \bm{p}_0 + \bm{V}\bm{\pi}_{1:M}$, we define the normalized consensus coordinate vector
\begin{equation}
\bm{u}_0 \equiv \bm{V}^{-1}\bm{p}_0 \in \RR^M, \qquad \bm{V}^{-1}\bar{\bm{p}} = \bm{u}_0 + \bm{\pi}_{1:M}.
\end{equation}
Evaluating the product $\bm{\Sigma}_\pi^{-1} \bm{V}^{-1}\bar{\bm{p}}$ using Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_sigma_q_inverse} gives
\begin{equation}
\bm{\Sigma}_\pi^{-1}\bm{V}^{-1}\bar{\bm{p}} = \left( \bm{D}_\pi^{-1} + \frac{\bm{1}_M \bm{1}_M^\top}{\pi_0} \right) (\bm{u}_0 + \bm{\pi}_{1:M}) = \bm{D}_\pi^{-1}\bm{u}_0 + \frac{1 + \bm{1}_M^\top \bm{u}_0}{\pi_0} \bm{1}_M.
\label{eq:app_sigma_q_inv_V_inv_pbar}
\end{equation}
For the consensus sector ($k = 0$), the displacement is $\bm{p}_0 - \bar{\bm{p}} = -\bm{V}\bm{\pi}_{1:M}$, so Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_variational_weights_start} becomes
\begin{equation}
w_0^* = 1 + \bm{\pi}_{1:M}^\top \bm{\Sigma}_\pi^{-1} \bm{V}^{-1}\bar{\bm{p}} = 1 + \bm{1}_M^\top \bm{u}_0 + \frac{1 - \pi_0}{\pi_0} \big(1 + \bm{1}_M^\top \bm{u}_0\big) = \frac{1 + \bm{1}_M^\top \bm{V}^{-1}\bm{p}_0}{\pi_0}.
\label{eq:app_w0_exact}
\end{equation}
For directional sectors ($k \in \{1, \dots, M\}$), the displacement is $\bm{p}_k - \bar{\bm{p}} = \bm{V}(\bm{e}_k - \bm{\pi}_{1:M})$.
Using Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_w0_exact}, we obtain
\begin{equation}
w_k^* = w_0^* - \bm{e}_k^\top \left( \bm{D}_\pi^{-1}\bm{u}_0 + \frac{1 + \bm{1}_M^\top \bm{u}_0}{\pi_0} \bm{1}_M \right) = w_0^* - \left( \frac{u_{0,k}}{\pi_k} + w_0^* \right) = -\frac{(\bm{V}^{-1}\bm{p}_0)_k}{\pi_k}.
\label{eq:app_wk_exact}
\end{equation}
Direct substitution confirms $\sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k w_k^* = 1$ and $\sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k w_k^* \bm{p}_k = \bm{0}$.
Evaluating the minimal sampling overhead $C_S^* = \sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k (w_k^*)^2$ by substituting Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_weights_closed_form} yields Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_overhead_exact}, completing the proof.
\end{proof}

The closed-form weights in Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_weights_closed_form} reveal the physical mechanism of virtual error cancellation:
the consensus weight $w_0^* = w_I > 1$ amplifies the agreement sector, whereas the directional weights $w_k^* = w_Q < 0$ subtract error contributions from contested shots.
This mechanism contrasts with physical probabilistic error cancellation (PEC).
In physical PEC, auxiliary physical circuits with artificially amplified noise are executed to sample quasi-probability distributions over quantum operations, inflating runtime and requiring explicit physical gate modifications.
In the dual-decoder architecture, virtual error cancellation requires no auxiliary circuits, instead assigning negative weights in classical software post-processing to shots identified as error-prone by decoder disagreement (Fig.~\ref{fig:dual_decoder}d).
In the ideal postselection limit where consensus errors vanish identically ($\bm{p}_0 \to \bm{0}$), the weights reduce to $w_0^* \to 1/\pi_0$ and $w_k^* \to 0$, recovering the shot-rejection overhead $C_S^* \to 1/\pi_0 = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k / \pi_0$ of discarding contested shots; when $\bm{p}_0 \ne \bm{0}$, non-zero negative weights $w_k^* < 0$ cancel the consensus bias without discarding data.

\subsubsection{Fault-Tolerant Logical Overhead Scaling}

\begin{theorem}[Universal Fault-Tolerant Overhead Scaling in Matrix Quadratic Form]
\label{thm:app_asymptotic_overhead}
In a fault-tolerant surface code below threshold ($p < p_{\mathrm{th}}$) with $K = M+1$ sectors resolving $M = 4^n - 1$ channel modes, and sector parameters satisfying Hypotheses~H1--H3, the sampling overhead of the minimal resolving protocol satisfies a universal matrix quadratic scaling law,
\begin{equation}
C_S^* = 1 + (\bm{V}^{-1}\bar{\bm{p}}_L)^\top \operatorname{diag}(\bm{\pi})^{-1} (\bm{V}^{-1}\bar{\bm{p}}_L) + \mathcal{O}(p_L^2) = 1 + \mathcal{O}\left(\frac{p_L}{\sigma_{\min}(\bm{V})}\right) = 1 + \mathcal{O}(p_L),
\label{eq:app_overhead_linear_scaling}
\end{equation}
where $\bm{\pi} \equiv (\pi_1, \dots, \pi_M)^\top \in \RR^M$ is the directional sector probability vector and $\bar{\bm{p}}_L \equiv \sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k \bm{p}_k \in \RR^M$ is the unmitigated logical error vector.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
In a fault-tolerant quantum error-correcting code below threshold ($p < p_{\mathrm{th}}$), directional sectors isolate syndrome configurations that herald elevated logical failure susceptibility along channel modes $k \in \{1, \dots, M\}$, with conditional error rates $p_{k,k} \sim \mathcal{O}(1)$.
Because logical faults require an error chain of weight at least $\lceil d/2 \rceil = (d+1)/2$, directional sector probabilities scale with the unmitigated logical error rates as $\pi_k = \Theta(p_{L,k}) = \mathcal{O}((p/p_{\mathrm{th}})^{(d+1)/2})$ for single-mode errors and $\pi_k = \mathcal{O}(p_L)$ for correlated multi-fault configurations, giving total directional probability mass $\pi_{\mathrm{dir}} \equiv \sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k = \Theta(p_L)$ and reference probability $\pi_0 = 1 - \pi_{\mathrm{dir}} = 1 - \mathcal{O}(p_L)$.
The coordinate vector $\bm{u}_0 \equiv \bm{V}^{-1}\bm{p}_0 \in \RR^M$ satisfies componentwise the per-channel bound $u_{0,k} = \mathcal{O}(\alpha \pi_k)$ of Hypothesis~H2.

Expanding the first term of the exact rational overhead in Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_overhead_exact} in powers of $\pi_{\mathrm{dir}}$ and $\bm{u}_0$ gives
\begin{align}
\frac{(1 + \bm{1}_M^\top \bm{u}_0)^2}{\pi_0} &= \frac{1 + 2\,\bm{1}_M^\top \bm{u}_0 + \mathcal{O}(\|\bm{u}_0\|^2)}{1 - \pi_{\mathrm{dir}}} \nonumber \\
&= \big(1 + \pi_{\mathrm{dir}} + \mathcal{O}(\pi_{\mathrm{dir}}^2)\big)\big(1 + 2\,\bm{1}_M^\top \bm{u}_0 + \mathcal{O}(\|\bm{u}_0\|^2)\big) \nonumber \\
&= 1 + \sum_{k=1}^M \big(\pi_k + 2 u_{0,k}\big) + \mathcal{O}(p_L^2).
\end{align}
Combining this expansion with the directional sector variance penalty $\sum_{k=1}^M u_{0,k}^2 / \pi_k$, the total sampling overhead evaluates to
\begin{align}
C_S^* &= 1 + \sum_{k=1}^M \left( \pi_k + 2 u_{0,k} + \frac{u_{0,k}^2}{\pi_k} \right) + \mathcal{O}(p_L^2) \nonumber \\
&= 1 + \sum_{k=1}^M \frac{(\pi_k + u_{0,k})^2}{\pi_k} + \mathcal{O}(p_L^2) \nonumber \\
&= 1 + (\bm{\pi} + \bm{u}_0)^\top \operatorname{diag}(\bm{\pi})^{-1} (\bm{\pi} + \bm{u}_0) + \mathcal{O}(p_L^2).
\label{eq:app_overhead_sum_completed_squares}
\end{align}
We now prove the exact vector sum identity connecting $\bm{\pi} + \bm{u}_0$ to the unmitigated error vector $\bar{\bm{p}}_L$.
By definition of the unconditional mean error vector in $\RR^M$,
\begin{equation}
\bar{\bm{p}}_L = \sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k \bm{p}_k = \pi_0 \bm{p}_0 + \sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k \bm{p}_k = (1 - \pi_{\mathrm{dir}})\bm{p}_0 + \sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k (\bm{v}_k + \bm{p}_0) = \bm{p}_0 + \bm{V}\bm{\pi}.
\end{equation}
Multiplying both sides by the inverse displacement matrix $\bm{V}^{-1}$ yields the exact vector sum relation
\begin{equation}
\bm{V}^{-1}\bar{\bm{p}}_L = \bm{V}^{-1}\bm{p}_0 + \bm{\pi} = \bm{u}_0 + \bm{\pi}.
\label{eq:app_vector_sum_identity}
\end{equation}
Substituting Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_vector_sum_identity} into Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_overhead_sum_completed_squares} establishes the universal matrix quadratic scaling law
\begin{equation}
C_S^* = 1 + (\bm{V}^{-1}\bar{\bm{p}}_L)^\top \operatorname{diag}(\bm{\pi})^{-1} (\bm{V}^{-1}\bar{\bm{p}}_L) + \mathcal{O}(p_L^2).
\end{equation}
Evaluating the quadratic form coordinate by coordinate using the exact completed square of Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_vector_sum_identity} together with the per-channel leakage bound of Hypothesis~H2 gives
\begin{equation}
(\bm{V}^{-1}\bar{\bm{p}}_L)^\top \operatorname{diag}(\bm{\pi})^{-1} (\bm{V}^{-1}\bar{\bm{p}}_L) = \sum_{k=1}^M \frac{(\pi_k + u_{0,k})^2}{\pi_k} = \sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k \left(1 + \frac{u_{0,k}}{\pi_k}\right)^2 = \sum_{k=1}^M \mathcal{O}(\pi_k) = \mathcal{O}(p_L),
\end{equation}
where the last equality uses $\sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k = 1 - \pi_0 = \mathcal{O}(p_L)$ from Hypothesis~H3.
Each summand is controlled by its own sector probability, so the bound is independent of how the total directional weight is distributed among the $M$ channels and remains valid when $\pi_k$ spans several distinct powers of $p$.
This proves that $C_S^* = 1 + \mathcal{O}(p_L)$ holds universally without requiring coordinate decoupling.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uncoupled Check Basis Reduction and Scalar Completed Squares]
\label{cor:app_overhead_uncoupled}
When cross-axis channel leakage is small ($\|\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}}\|_\infty \ll 1$), the coordinate displacement matrix $\bm{V} \in \RR^{M \times M}$ is diagonally dominant with diagonal entries $V_{k,k} = p_{k,k} + \mathcal{O}(p_L)$, where $\alpha_k \equiv p_{k,0}/\bar{p}_{L,k} \in (0, 1)$ denotes the agreement error suppression ratio along channel mode $k$.
Along each channel mode $k \in \{1, \dots, M\}$, the directional probability, reference coordinate, and logical error rate satisfy the scalar completed-square identity
\begin{equation}
\pi_k + 2 u_{0,k} + \frac{u_{0,k}^2}{\pi_k} = \frac{(\pi_k + u_{0,k})^2}{\pi_k} = \frac{\bar{p}_{L,k}}{p_{k,k}(1 - \alpha_k)} + \mathcal{O}\big(\|\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}}\|_\infty p_L + p_L^2\big) = \eta_k \bar{p}_{L,k} + \mathcal{O}\big(\|\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}}\|_\infty p_L + p_L^2\big),
\label{eq:app_overhead_completed_square}
\end{equation}
with $\eta_k \equiv 1 / [p_{k,k}(1 - \alpha_k)]$.
Consequently, the sampling overhead reduces to the decoupled linear formula
\begin{equation}
C_S^* = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^M \eta_k \bar{p}_{L,k} + \mathcal{O}\big(\|\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}}\|_\infty p_L + p_L^2\big) = 1 + \mathcal{O}(p_L).
\label{eq:app_overhead_uncoupled_asymptotic}
\end{equation}
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
In the decoupled limit ($\|\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}}\|_\infty \ll 1$), the diagonal displacement entries are $V_{k,k} = p_{k,k} - p_{k,0} = p_{k,k} + \mathcal{O}(p_L)$.
Projecting the exact vector sum identity in Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_vector_sum_identity} onto coordinate axis $k$ yields
\begin{equation}
\pi_k + u_{0,k} = (\bm{V}^{-1}\bar{\bm{p}}_L)_k = \frac{\bar{p}_{L,k}}{p_{k,k}} + \mathcal{O}\big(\|\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}}\|_\infty p_L + p_L^2\big).
\end{equation}
By total probability along coordinate axis $k$, the directional sector probability expands as
\begin{equation}
\pi_k = \frac{\bar{p}_{L,k} - p_{k,0}}{p_{k,k} - p_{k,0}} + \mathcal{O}\big(\|\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}}\|_\infty p_L + p_L^2\big) = \frac{(1 - \alpha_k)\bar{p}_{L,k}}{p_{k,k}} + \mathcal{O}\big(\|\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}}\|_\infty p_L + p_L^2\big).
\end{equation}
Substituting $\pi_k + u_{0,k}$ and $\pi_k$ into the $k$-th term of the matrix quadratic form in Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_overhead_sum_completed_squares} gives
\begin{equation}
\frac{(\pi_k + u_{0,k})^2}{\pi_k} = \frac{\big[\bar{p}_{L,k} / p_{k,k}\big]^2}{(1 - \alpha_k)\bar{p}_{L,k} / p_{k,k}} + \mathcal{O}\big(\|\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}}\|_\infty p_L + p_L^2\big) = \eta_k \bar{p}_{L,k} + \mathcal{O}\big(\|\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}}\|_\infty p_L + p_L^2\big).
\end{equation}
Summing over all channel modes $k \in \{1, \dots, M\}$ yields Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_overhead_uncoupled_asymptotic}, completing the proof.
\end{proof}

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Residual Logical Error, Calibration Perturbations, and Distance Suppression}{Residual Logical Error, Calibration Perturbations, and Distance Suppression}}
\label{subsec:app_proof_distance_suppression}
\label{subsec:app_residual_error_distance_suppression}
\label{subsec:coupled_sector_derivations}
\label{sec:appendix_residual_error}

In this subsection, we analyze the sensitivity of the minimal $(M+1)$-sector virtual error cancellation protocol to parameter calibration errors and establish the agreement-sector distance suppression of residual logical errors.
Under calibrated sector probabilities $\hat{\pi}_k = \pi_k(1 + \varepsilon_{\pi,k})$ (with $\sum_{k=0}^M \hat{\pi}_k = 1$) and calibrated error centroids $\hat{\bm{p}}_k$ with components $\hat{p}_{i,k} = p_{i,k}(1 + \varepsilon_{i,k})$ and displacement matrix $\hat{\bm{V}} \equiv (\hat{\bm{p}}_1 - \hat{\bm{p}}_0, \dots, \hat{\bm{p}}_M - \hat{\bm{p}}_0)$, substituting the calibrated parameters into Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_weights_closed_form} defines the plug-in calibrated sector weights $\hat{w}_0 = (1 + \bm{1}_M^\top \hat{\bm{V}}^{-1}\hat{\bm{p}}_0)/\hat{\pi}_0$ and $\hat{w}_k = -(\hat{\bm{V}}^{-1}\hat{\bm{p}}_0)_k/\hat{\pi}_k$ for $k \in \{1, \dots, M\}$.
While $\sum_{k=0}^M \hat{\pi}_k \hat{w}_k = 1$ holds by construction, the physical ensemble mean weight on the evaluation syndrome stream is $\bar{w} \equiv \EE_{\bm{s}}[\hat{w}(\bm{s})] = \sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k \hat{w}_k$.
In the Pauli channel expansion of Eq.~\eqref{eq:channel_inversion_expanded}, applying raw weights $\hat{w}_k$ without enforcing unit mean when $\hat{\pi}_k \neq \pi_k$ would introduce an unprojected trace-preservation drift $1 - \bar{w} = \Theta(\varepsilon_{\max} p_L)$.
Because sector memberships $\mathcal{S}_k$ are observed directly in classical post-processing, the trace-preservation condition $\EE_{\bm{s}}[w(\bm{s})] = 1$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:pauli_cancellation_constraint} is enforced on the filter $w(\bm{s})$ in the linear estimator $\hat{\langle O \rangle}_{\mathrm{VEC}} = N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^N w(\bm{s}_i) m_i$ either by normalizing the calibrated weights by their ensemble mean, $w(\bm{s}) = \hat{w}(\bm{s})/\bar{w}$ (implemented on an $N$-shot evaluation sample via the empirical mean weight $\bar{w}_N = N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^N \hat{w}(\bm{s}_i)$), or by evaluating $\hat{\pi}_k$ directly from the empirical sector frequencies of the evaluation syndrome stream so that $\hat{\pi}_k = \pi_k$ ($\varepsilon_{\pi,k} = 0$) and $\bar{w} = 1$ identically (analogous to evaluating the sample mean on the syndrome stream in Subsection~\ref{subsubsec:app_cross_moment_decomp}).
Under either enforcement of trace preservation, the residual logical error vector across the $M$ Pauli channels is $\bm{p}_{\mathrm{res}} \equiv \bar{w}^{-1}\sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k \hat{w}_k \bm{p}_k$.
We now derive the exact first-order differential of $\bm{p}_{\mathrm{res}}$ under calibration perturbations, prove uniform shift invariance, and establish Theorem~\ref{thm:app_distance_suppression}.

\begin{proposition}[Exact First-Order Residual Differential and Binary Reduction]
\label{thm:app_exact_differential}
Under parameter perturbations $\hat{\pi}_k = \pi_k(1 + \varepsilon_{\pi,k})$ and relative centroid deviations $\hat{p}_{i,k} = p_{i,k}(1 + \varepsilon_{i,k})$ for $K = M + 1$, the first-order differential of the normalized residual logical error vector $\bm{p}_{\mathrm{res}} \equiv \bar{w}^{-1}\sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k \hat{w}_k \bm{p}_k$ along Pauli axis $k \in \{1, \dots, M\}$ decouples in the absence of cross-talk ($\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}} \to \bm{0}$),
\begin{equation}
dp_{\mathrm{res}, k} = \frac{p_{k,0} p_{k,k}}{p_{k,k} - p_{k,0}} \left( \frac{\varepsilon_{\pi,k}}{1 - \pi_k} - \varepsilon_{0,k} + \varepsilon_{k,k} \right) + \mathcal{O}\big(p_{k,0}(1 - \pi_0) \|\bm{\varepsilon}_\pi\|_\infty\big) + \mathcal{O}\big(\|\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}}\|_\infty \|\bm{p}_0\| \|\bm{\varepsilon}\|\big),
\label{eq:app_dp_res_closed_form}
\end{equation}
where $\varepsilon_{0,k} \equiv \varepsilon_{k,0}$ and $\varepsilon_{k,k}$ denote the relative perturbations of the reference and directional error rates $p_{k,0}$ and $p_{k,k}$ along axis $k$.
For a single error process ($K = 2$, with agreement probability $1-\pi$, uncontested error $\pa \equiv p_{k,0}$, contested error $\pc \equiv p_{k,k}$, and relative uncertainties $\varepsilon_a, \varepsilon_c, \varepsilon_m$), the calibrated weights yield normalized residual error
\begin{equation}
p_{\mathrm{res}}(\bm{\varepsilon}) = \frac{\frac{1 - \pi}{1 - \hat{\pi}}\hat{p}_c \pa - \frac{\pi}{\hat{\pi}}\hat{p}_a \pc}{\frac{1 - \pi}{1 - \hat{\pi}}\hat{p}_c - \frac{\pi}{\hat{\pi}}\hat{p}_a},
\label{eq:app_pres_rational_estimators}
\end{equation}
in which the singular factor $(\hat{p}_c - \hat{p}_a)^{-1}$ cancels between numerator and denominator, reducing Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_dp_res_closed_form} to the binary differential
\begin{equation}
dp_{\mathrm{res}} = \frac{\pa \pc}{\pc - \pa} \left( \frac{\varepsilon_m}{1 - \pi} - \varepsilon_a + \varepsilon_c \right).
\label{eq:app_dp_res_binary_reduction}
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The plug-in calibrated filter weights $\hat{\bm{w}} = (\hat{w}_0, \dots, \hat{w}_M)^\top$ satisfy $\hat{w}_0 = (1 + \bm{1}_M^\top \hat{\bm{V}}^{-1}\hat{\bm{p}}_0)/\hat{\pi}_0$ and $\hat{w}_k = -(\hat{\bm{V}}^{-1}\hat{\bm{p}}_0)_k/\hat{\pi}_k$ for $k \in \{1, \dots, M\}$.
Enforcing the trace-preservation condition $\EE_{\bm{s}}[w(\bm{s})] = 1$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:pauli_cancellation_constraint} via the normalized sector weights $\hat{w}_k / \bar{w}$ yields the residual logical error vector
\begin{equation}
\bm{p}_{\mathrm{res}} \equiv \frac{1}{\bar{w}} \sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k \hat{w}_k \bm{p}_k, \qquad \bar{w} \equiv \sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k \hat{w}_k.
\label{eq:app_p_res_def}
\end{equation}
At zero calibration error ($\hat{\bm{w}} = \bm{w}^*$), the unnormalized numerator vanishes ($\sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k w_k^* \bm{p}_k = \bm{0}$) and $\bar{w} = \sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k w_k^* = 1$, giving $\bm{p}_{\mathrm{res}} = \bm{0}$.
Differentiating Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_p_res_def} around $\hat{\bm{w}} = \bm{w}^*$ by the quotient rule, the variation of $1/\bar{w}$ multiplies $\sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k w_k^* \bm{p}_k = \bm{0}$ and vanishes to first order, so $d\bm{p}_{\mathrm{res}} = \sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k (d\hat{w}_k)\bm{p}_k$.
Substituting $\bm{p}_k = \bm{p}_0 + \bm{v}_k$ ($k \in \{1, \dots, M\}$) with $d\bar{w} = \sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k d\hat{w}_k$ then projects $d\bar{w}$ onto the reference centroid $\bm{p}_0$,
\begin{equation}
d\bm{p}_{\mathrm{res}} = \sum_{k=0}^M \pi_k (d\hat{w}_k) \bm{p}_k = (d\bar{w}) \bm{p}_0 + \sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k (d\hat{w}_k) \bm{v}_k,
\label{eq:app_dp_res_step1}
\end{equation}
where $d\hat{w}_k \equiv \hat{w}_k - w_k^*$.
Differentiating the weight relations $\hat{\pi}_k \hat{w}_k = -\hat{u}_{0,k}$ and $\hat{\pi}_0 \hat{w}_0 = 1 + \bm{1}_M^\top \hat{\bm{u}}_0$ gives
\begin{align}
\pi_k d\hat{w}_k &= -d u_{0,k} + \varepsilon_{\pi,k} u_{0,k} + \mathcal{O}\big(\|\bm{\varepsilon}\|^2\big) \qquad (k \in \{1, \dots, M\}), \\
\pi_0 d\hat{w}_0 &= \bm{1}_M^\top d\bm{u}_0 - \varepsilon_{\pi,0}\big(1 + \bm{1}_M^\top \bm{u}_0\big) + \mathcal{O}\big(\|\bm{\varepsilon}\|^2\big),
\end{align}
yielding $d\bar{w} = -\varepsilon_{\pi,0} + \sum_{k=1}^M (\varepsilon_{\pi,k} - \varepsilon_{\pi,0}) u_{0,k} + \mathcal{O}(\|\bm{\varepsilon}\|^2)$.
The coordinate variation $d\bm{u}_0 \equiv d(\bm{V}^{-1}\bm{p}_0)$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
d\bm{u}_0 = \bm{V}^{-1}\left[ (1 + \bm{1}_M^\top \bm{u}_0) d\hat{\bm{p}}_0 - \sum_{k=1}^M u_{0,k} d\hat{\bm{p}}_k \right].
\end{equation}
Multiplying by $\bm{V}$ and substituting into Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_dp_res_step1} together with $\bm{v}_k = \bm{p}_k - \bm{p}_0$ and $(1 + \bm{1}_M^\top \bm{u}_0)\bm{p}_0 = \sum_{k=1}^M u_{0,k}\bm{p}_k$ yields the exact multi-channel differential $d\bm{p}_{\mathrm{res}} = -(1 + \bm{1}_M^\top \bm{u}_0)d\hat{\bm{p}}_0 + \sum_{k=1}^M u_{0,k}[d\hat{\bm{p}}_k + (\varepsilon_{\pi,k} - \varepsilon_{\pi,0})\bm{p}_k]$.
Using $\pi_0 \varepsilon_{\pi,0} = -\sum_{j=1}^M \pi_j \varepsilon_{\pi,j}$ so that $\varepsilon_{\pi,k} - \varepsilon_{\pi,0} = \varepsilon_{\pi,k}/(1 - \pi_k) + \mathcal{O}((1 - \pi_0)\|\bm{\varepsilon}_\pi\|_\infty)$, the variation along each Pauli axis decouples when $\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}} \to \bm{0}$, yielding Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_dp_res_closed_form}, with leading cross-axis correction $\frac{V_{13}}{V_{33}} p_{3,0}(-\varepsilon_{1,1} + \varepsilon_{1,3} - \varepsilon_{\pi,1} + \varepsilon_{\pi,3})$.
For the single-process binary reduction ($K = 2$), substituting the calibrated weights $\hat{w}_a = \frac{\hat{p}_c}{\hat{p}_c - \hat{p}_a}\frac{1}{1-\hat{\pi}}$ and $\hat{w}_c = -\frac{\hat{p}_a}{\hat{p}_c - \hat{p}_a}\frac{1}{\hat{\pi}}$ into $p_{\mathrm{res}} = [(1-\pi)\hat{w}_a \pa + \pi\hat{w}_c \pc] / [(1-\pi)\hat{w}_a + \pi\hat{w}_c]$ shows that the factor $(\hat{p}_c - \hat{p}_a)^{-1}$ cancels identically, establishing Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_pres_rational_estimators}.
Because the unnormalized numerator vanishes at $\bm{\varepsilon} = \bm{0}$ and $\bar{w}(\bm{0}) = 1$, the quotient rule confirms that $\bm{\nabla} p_{\mathrm{res}}|_{\bm{0}}$ equals the gradient of the numerator, which evaluates directly to Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_dp_res_binary_reduction}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Uniform Shift Invariance]
\label{lem:app_uniform_shift_invariance}
If calibration errors induce a common-mode relative shift between reference and directional estimates along axis $k$ ($\varepsilon_{0,k} = \varepsilon_{k,k} = \varepsilon$, with exact mismatch calibration $\varepsilon_{\pi,k} = 0$), the differential vanishes identically in the absence of cross-talk,
\begin{equation}
-\varepsilon_{0,k} + \varepsilon_{k,k} = -\varepsilon + \varepsilon = 0 \implies dp_{\mathrm{res}, k} = 0 + \mathcal{O}\big(\|\Delta\bm{V}_{\mathrm{off}}\|_\infty \|\bm{p}_0\| \|\bm{\varepsilon}\|\big).
\end{equation}
Uniform calibration misestimations do not corrupt the unbiasing accuracy to first order.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Directly follows by substituting $\varepsilon_{0,k} = \varepsilon_{k,k} = \varepsilon$ and $\varepsilon_{\pi,k} = 0$ into Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_dp_res_closed_form}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Residual Error Calibration Suppression via Agreement-Sector Projection]
\label{thm:app_distance_suppression}
In the fault-tolerant regime below threshold ($p < p_{\mathrm{th}}$) for $K = M + 1$, because $p_{k,0} \ll p_{k,k} \sim \mathcal{O}(1)$ with $p_{k,k} \ge 1/4$, the prefactor in Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_dp_res_closed_form} simplifies to
\begin{equation}
\frac{p_{k,0} p_{k,k}}{p_{k,k} - p_{k,0}} = p_{k,0}\big(1 + \mathcal{O}(p_{k,0})\big).
\end{equation}
Taking the Euclidean norm across all $M$ Pauli coordinates yields
\begin{equation}
\|\bm{p}_{\mathrm{res}}\| = \mathcal{O}\big(\varepsilon_{\max} \|\bm{p}_0\|\big) = \mathcal{O}\big(\alpha \varepsilon_{\max} \|\bar{\bm{p}}\|\big),
\label{eq:app_distance_suppression_final}
\end{equation}
where $\varepsilon_{\max} \equiv \max_{i,k} \{ |\varepsilon_{\pi,k}|, |\varepsilon_{i,k}| \}$ and $\alpha \equiv \|\bm{p}_0\| / \|\bar{\bm{p}}\| \in (0, 1)$ denotes the consensus agreement error suppression ratio (Hypothesis~H3).
Consequently, the minimal resolving protocol ($K = M + 1$) suppresses residual calibration bias by the agreement factor $\alpha < 1$ relative to uncoupled estimation.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Below threshold, the reference error rates satisfy $p_{k,0} = \Theta(p_L) = \Theta((p/p_{\mathrm{th}})^{(d+1)/2})$ and $\|\bm{p}_0\| = \alpha \|\bar{\bm{p}}\|$ with $\alpha \in (0, 1)$, while directional rates satisfy $p_{k,k} \sim \mathcal{O}(1)$ with $p_{k,k} \ge 1/4 - \mathcal{O}(p_L)$.
The prefactor evaluates to
\begin{equation}
\frac{p_{k,0} p_{k,k}}{p_{k,k} - p_{k,0}} = \frac{p_{k,0}}{1 - p_{k,0}/p_{k,k}} = p_{k,0}\big(1 + \mathcal{O}(p_{k,0})\big).
\end{equation}
Because enforcing trace preservation projects the visibility shift $d\bar{w}$ onto $\bm{p}_0$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_dp_res_step1} (or sets $d\bar{w} = 0$ identically when $\hat{\pi}_k = \pi_k$ uses the empirical evaluation-stream frequencies), bounding the bracketed relative error terms in Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_dp_res_closed_form} by $\varepsilon_{\max}$ and taking the $\ell_2$ norm across all $M$ coordinates yields $\|\bm{p}_{\mathrm{res}}\| = \mathcal{O}(\varepsilon_{\max} \|\bm{p}_0\|)$.
Since the unconditional error vector satisfies $\|\bar{\bm{p}}\| = \Theta(p_L) = \Theta((p/p_{\mathrm{th}})^{(d+1)/2})$ and the agreement error suppression ratio satisfies $\alpha \equiv \|\bm{p}_0\|/\|\bar{\bm{p}}\| \in (0, 1)$, we obtain $\|\bm{p}_{\mathrm{res}}\| = \mathcal{O}(\alpha \varepsilon_{\max} \|\bar{\bm{p}}\|)$, completing the proof.
\end{proof}

This result demonstrates two physical advantages over alternative mitigation and postselection strategies.
First, discarding disagreement shots via postselection leaves an unmitigated systematic error equal to the consensus centroid $\bm{p}_0 = \alpha \bar{\bm{p}}$, accompanied by an exponential sample rejection rate.
The four-sector filter eliminates this systematic bias without shot rejection, leaving only the calibration-induced residual error $\|\bm{p}_{\mathrm{res}}\| = \mathcal{O}(\alpha \varepsilon_{\max} \|\bar{\bm{p}}\|)$, which is suppressed by the relative calibration precision $\varepsilon_{\max} \ll 1$ below the postselection floor.
Second, in physical probabilistic error cancellation (PEC), calibration inaccuracies in physical inversion gates directly yield residual logical errors scaling with the unmitigated rate, $\|\bm{p}_{\mathrm{res}}^{\mathrm{PEC}}\| = \mathcal{O}(\varepsilon_{\max} \|\bar{\bm{p}}\|)$.
In contrast, four-sector mitigation achieves an additional suppression factor $\alpha \equiv \|\bm{p}_0\|/\|\bar{\bm{p}}\| \ll 1$, because errors in the calibrated filter weights act only on consensus-projected coordinates.

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Microscopic Derivation of the Dual-Decoder Error Asymmetry Ratio and Conditional Centroids}{Microscopic Derivation of the Dual-Decoder Error Asymmetry Ratio and Conditional Centroids}}
\label{subsec:app_decoder_asymmetry_derivation}

We now provide the microscopic derivation connecting the dual-decoder error asymmetry ratio $r_j$ to the underlying logical error rates of the real-time decoder $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{rt}}$ and offline reference decoder $\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{off}}$.
Let $E_{\mathrm{rt}, j}, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} \in \{0, 1\}$ denote the binary indicator variables for logical errors along check network $j \in \{X, Z\}$ after applying real-time and offline corrections, respectively.
Disagreement along check network $j$ corresponds to the binary indicator event $\chi_j^{\mathrm{dd}} = 1$, where $\chi_j^{\mathrm{dd}} \equiv \bm{1}[\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{rt}, j}(\bm{s}) \neq \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{off}, j}(\bm{s})]$.
This event decomposes into two disjoint physical outcomes,
\begin{equation}
P(\chi_j^{\mathrm{dd}} = 1) = P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 1, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 0) + P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 0, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 1) + \mathcal{O}(p_L^2).
\end{equation}
The unmitigated logical error rates of the two decoders along check network $j$ are given by the marginal expectations
\begin{align}
p_{L,j} &= P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 1) = P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 1, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 0) + P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 1, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 1), \\
p_{L,j}' &= P(E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 1) = P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 0, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 1) + P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 1, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 1).
\end{align}
In the consensus sector ($\chi_j^{\mathrm{dd}} = 0$), both decoders output identical logical corrections, so logical errors occur only when both decoders fail simultaneously on the same shot.
The conditional agreement error rate satisfies
\begin{equation}
p_{I,j} \equiv P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 1 \mid \chi_j^{\mathrm{dd}} = 0) = \frac{P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 1, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 1)}{P(\chi_j^{\mathrm{dd}} = 0)} = P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 1, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 1) + \mathcal{O}(p_L^2),
\end{equation}
because the agreement probability is $P(\chi_j^{\mathrm{dd}} = 0) = 1 - \mathcal{O}(p_L)$.
Subtracting the joint error rate from the marginal error rates yields
\begin{align}
P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 1, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 0) &= p_{L,j} - p_{I,j} + \mathcal{O}(p_L^2), \\
P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 0, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 1) &= p_{L,j}' - p_{I,j} + \mathcal{O}(p_L^2).
\end{align}
The decoder error asymmetry ratio is defined as the quotient of these two disjoint error probabilities,
\begin{equation}
r_j \equiv \frac{P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 0, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 1)}{P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 1, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 0)} = \frac{p_{L,j}' - p_{I,j}}{p_{L,j} - p_{I,j}} + \mathcal{O}(p_L).
\label{eq:app_asymmetry_ratio_derivation}
\end{equation}
The conditional error probability of the real-time decoder in the contested sector ($\chi_j^{\mathrm{dd}} = 1$) is
\begin{equation}
p_{X,j} \equiv P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 1 \mid \chi_j^{\mathrm{dd}} = 1) = \frac{P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 1, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 0)}{P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 1, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 0) + P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 0, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 1)}.
\end{equation}
Dividing numerator and denominator by $P(E_{\mathrm{rt}, j} = 1, \, E_{\mathrm{off}, j} = 0)$ yields
\begin{equation}
p_{X,j} = \frac{1}{1 + r_j} + \mathcal{O}(p_L).
\label{eq:app_pX_asymmetry_relation}
\end{equation}
Substituting Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_pX_asymmetry_relation} into the single-channel overhead slope formula $\eta_j = 1/[p_{X,j}(1 - \alpha_j)]$ gives Eq.~\eqref{eq:dd_overhead_eta_formula},
\begin{equation}
\eta_j^{\mathrm{dd}} = \frac{1 + r_j}{1 - \alpha_j} + \mathcal{O}(p_L).
\label{eq:app_eta_asymmetry_relation}
\end{equation}
This establishes the operational interpolation of the dual-decoder overhead slope $\bar{\eta}^{\mathrm{dd}} \in [1, 2]$ between two physical limits:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Ideal offline oracle limit:} As the offline decoder approaches an ideal heralding oracle ($p_{L,j}' \to 0$), the offline error probability vanishes and yields $r_j \to 0$.
In this limit, the contested error probability reaches certainty ($p_{X,j} \to 1$), and the sampling overhead slope approaches $\eta_j^{\mathrm{dd}} \to 1/(1 - \alpha_j) \approx 1$.
\item \emph{Symmetric degeneracy limit:} When the offline decoder provides the same marginal accuracy as the real-time decoder on contested shots ($p_{L,j}' \approx p_{L,j}$), the asymmetry ratio approaches unity ($r_j \to 1$).
In this limit, the contested error rate reduces to the MWPM tie-break baseline $p_{X,j} \to 1/2$, and the overhead slope approaches $\eta_j^{\mathrm{dd}} \to 2/(1 - \alpha_j) \approx 2$.
\end{enumerate}

