\begin{abstract}
    We determine the two-way quantum and secret-key capacities of the pure-loss channel under an average input photon-number constraint. For transmissivity $\eta$ and photon budget $N$, both capacities equal $g(N)-g((1-\eta)N)$, where $g$ is the entropy of a thermal state. This coincides with the rate achieved by the known reverse coherent information of a two-mode squeezed vacuum. Our result provides the last missing capacity formula for the celebrated pure-loss channel, completing the picture of bosonic quantum communication under pure loss.
\end{abstract}

\section{Main result}
\label{sec:main}

The single-mode pure-loss channel $\cL_\eta$, with transmissivity
$\eta\in[0,1]$, mixes the input with a vacuum environment on a beam
splitter~\cite{HW01,Weed12}. We use its dilation
\begin{equation}
 V_\eta\ket{k}
 =\sum_{j=0}^{k}\sqrt{\binom{k}{j}\eta^{k-j}(1-\eta)^j}
   \ket{k-j}_B\ket{j}_E,
 \qquad
 \cL_\eta(\rho)=\Tr_E V_\eta\rho V_\eta^\dagger.
 \label{eq:loss}
\end{equation}
Thus $j$ is the number of photons lost. The complementary channel is
$\cL_{1-\eta}$, up to the choice of an environment phase.

We consider the standard energy-constrained, two-way-assisted communication
model~\cite{DSW18,MLG25}. Alice and Bob start with local states and may share
public randomness, but no entanglement or secret key. They alternate $n$
uses of $\cL_\eta$ with arbitrary local operations and classical
communication (LOCC)~\cite{CLMOW14}. At each fixed blocklength, a code
uses finitely many local rounds and finite or countable classical message
alphabets; no uniform bound on the number of rounds is imposed as $n$ grows.
All operations act on trace-class states on separable Hilbert spaces;
their adjoints are normal.
The classical channel is authenticated and public.
Eve receives the
channel environments and the public transcript. The only energy constraint
is
\begin{equation}
 \frac1n\sum_{i=1}^{n}\Tr\!\left[\nhat\,\bar\rho_{S_i}\right]\le N,
 \qquad \nhat=a^\dagger a,
 \label{eq:energy-constraint}
\end{equation}
where $\bar\rho_{S_i}$ is the unconditional input state at use $i$:
previous measurement outcomes have already been averaged over. Local
ancillas and memories are unrestricted.

The capacities $Q_2(\cL_\eta,N)$ and $K(\cL_\eta,N)$ are the supremal
asymptotic rates of entanglement and secret-key generation, respectively,
with error tending to zero. Rates are measured in ebits and secret bits per
channel use. With free classical communication, entanglement generation and
quantum transmission have the same capacity by
teleportation~\cite{BBC93,DSW18}. For secret-key generation, we use the standard correctness and secrecy
error~\cite{PR22}. For entanglement generation, the error is the
infidelity with a maximally entangled state. Both errors are evaluated
on the unconditional output.

\begin{theorem}[Energy-constrained two-way capacities]
\label{thm:main}
For every $\eta\in[0,1]$ and every finite $N\ge0$,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\quad
 Q_2(\cL_\eta,N)=K(\cL_\eta,N)
 =g(N)-g\bigl((1-\eta)N\bigr),
 \quad}
 \label{eq:main}
\end{equation}
where $g(x)=(x+1)\log_2(x+1)-x\log_2x$ and $0\log_2 0=0$.
The optimization includes all adaptive LOCC protocols satisfying
\eqref{eq:energy-constraint}.
\end{theorem}

Throughout, logarithms are in base $2$, and all Hilbert spaces are
separable. We write $S(\rho)=-\Tr\rho\log_2\rho$ for von Neumann
entropy and $H(p)=-\sum_a p_a\log_2p_a$ for Shannon entropy. Identity
maps on untouched systems are implicit. We abbreviate
\begin{equation}
 q:=1-\eta,
 \qquad f_\eta(x):=g(x)-g(qx).
 \label{eq:f}
\end{equation}
The formula gives $0$ at $N=0$ or $\eta=0$, and $g(N)$ at $\eta=1$.
For $0\le\eta<1$, it also gives
$\lim_{N\to\infty}f_\eta(N)=-\log_2(1-\eta)$, recovering the
unconstrained capacity~\cite{PLOB17}. Earlier upper bounds for two-way
bosonic communication were obtained from squashed entanglement;
see~\cite{TGW14,GEW16,DSW18}. The achievable function $f_\eta$ comes from
reverse reconciliation and reverse coherent
information~\cite{PGBL09,GPLS09}.

Figure~\ref{fig:capacities-vs-eta} compares these rates with the other
capacities of the pure-loss channel at $N=1$. For $0<\eta\le1/2$, the
unassisted quantum and private classical capacities vanish~\cite{WHG12,WQ18},
whereas $Q_2$ and $K$ remain positive. At $\eta=1/2$, for example,
$Q_2=K\simeq0.6226$.

