\title{
    Markov length can diverge in systems whose universal physics is  spatially Markovian   
}

\author{Yu-Hsueh Chen} 
\affiliation{Department of Physics, University of California at San Diego, La Jolla, California 92093, USA} 
\affiliation{Kavli Institute for Theoretical Physics, University of California, Santa Barbara, California 93106, USA}
\author{Tarun Grover} 
\affiliation{Department of Physics, University of California at San Diego, La Jolla, California 92093, USA}


\begin{abstract}
Conditional mutual information (CMI) probes spatial non-Markovianity and plays a central role in a recently developed framework for defining mixed-state phases based on local reversibility. This framework sharply distinguishes exponentially decaying from algebraically decaying CMI, since the latter obstructs equivalence to a state with finite Markov length. Here we show that states that flow to the same
renormalization-group (RG) fixed point can nevertheless exhibit qualitatively different CMI. Starting from a critical Gibbs state of a finite-range local classical Hamiltonian,
whose CMI vanishes beyond the interaction range, we show that an RG-irrelevant perturbation that breaks
detailed balance generically generates algebraically decaying CMI.  
{Crucially, these power-law tails are cutoff-suppressed: although they lead to an infinite Markov length at every fixed lattice cutoff, they vanish as a positive power of the cutoff in the continuum limit.}
We construct solvable models that exhibit this phenomenon and predict that the Ising-symmetric critical point of Toom's cellular automata generically exhibits such behavior. We also find analogous cutoff-suppressed CMI in the high-temperature paramagnetic phase of the long-range Ising paramagnet, in contrast with the genuine power-law CMI at the finite-temperature critical point in this same  model that survives the continuum limit. We also derive a general result that in one spatial dimension, CMI decays faster than the inverse square of the buffer length if and only if the long-distance physics is Markovian.

\end{abstract}
\maketitle

\textbf{Introduction.} 
The renormalization group (RG) allows one to define phases and phase transitions as equivalence classes of states that lie in the basin of attraction of distinct RG fixed points. States that flow to the same fixed point are expected to then share the same long-distance and long-time properties (``universal properties'') as the corresponding RG fixed point. For example, if one perturbs an RG fixed point with an irrelevant perturbation, then the leading scaling behavior of correlation functions is expected to remain unchanged. In this paper we will show that this expectation requires refinement for \textit{conditional correlations}, such as conditional mutual information (CMI). In particular, if one perturbs a dynamical RG fixed point whose steady state is a Gibbs state of a local Hamiltonian (`local Gibbs' for short) with power-law correlations, then we find that an \textit{irrelevant} detailed-balance-breaking perturbation generically causes the CMI, which is initially zero~\cite{clifford1971markov,leifer2008quantum,brown2012quantum}, to acquire a power-law decay. Crucially, the nature of such power laws is quite different from the power laws of ordinary correlation functions: they are suppressed by powers of the lattice cutoff and disappear in the scaling limit, where the ratio of the lattice cutoff to all macroscopic
length scales is sent to zero, thereby recovering the
spatially Markovian behavior of the fixed point.
This fingerprint allows one to distinguish such unusual power-law CMI from CMI that arises in systems such as 1+1-D directed percolation criticality, where the effective description of the system is genuinely non-Markovian and where the power-law CMI does not vanish as the lattice cutoff is sent to zero~\cite{chen2025local}.

To set up the problem, consider a $d$-dimensional system partitioned into regions $A$, $B$, and $C$, with $B$ shielding $A$ from $C$. We will primarily use the
annular geometry of Ref.~\cite{sang2025stability}: $A$ is
a hypercube of linear size $l$, $A\cup B$ has
linear size $l+2l_B$, and
$C=\overline{A\cup B}$ [see Fig.~\ref{fig:geometries}(a)]. The CMI $I(A:C|B) = S(AB) + S(BC) - S(ABC) - S(B)$, where $S(X)$ is the von Neumann entropy of the state in region $X$, measures correlations between $A$
and $C$ that are not mediated by $B$.  
The Markov length $\xi_M$ is defined through $\textrm{CMI}\sim e^{-l_B/\xi_M}$ in the large-$l_B$ limit. Therefore, any subexponential decay in
$l_B$ corresponds to $\xi_M=\infty$.

The distinction between exponentially decaying and algebraic CMI plays a crucial role in defining mixed-state phases of matter~\cite{coser2019classification,rakovszky2024defining,sang2024mixed,sang2025stability,sang2025mixed}. As discussed in Refs.\cite{sang2025stability,sang2025mixed}, if a density matrix $\rho_1$ can be converted to another density matrix $\rho_2$ via a short-time local Lindblad evolution such that CMI continues to decay exponentially throughout the evolution, then it is natural to regard them as belonging to the same phase of matter since the exponential decay of CMI implies that one can find another short-time local Lindblad evolution that converts $\rho_2$ to $\rho_1$. Further, if $\rho_1$ has power-law CMI while $\rho_2$ has exponentially decaying CMI, then there cannot be a locally reversible short-depth channel connecting the two states~\cite{jinmin2026universal}.

However, as we alluded to above, not all power-law CMI has the same status. We distinguish
\emph{continuum-surviving} power-law CMI, which remains
finite in the continuum scaling limit, from
\emph{cutoff-suppressed} power-law CMI, whose amplitude
vanishes as a positive power of the lattice cutoff $a$. To make this distinction precise, let's assume that we are close to a scale-invariant fixed point so that there exists a crossover length scale $\xi_{\rm cross} \sim a |p|^{-1/\omega}$ defined by the tuning parameter $p$ with RG eigenvalue $\omega$. 
For a relevant perturbation, $\omega > 0$,  this length scale will approach infinity, i.e., $\xi_{\rm cross } \gg l, l_B$,
	 whereas for an irrelevant perturbation, $\omega < 0$, which is the case of interest here, it remains of the order of the lattice spacing \footnote{We emphasize that, in general, $\xi_M \neq \xi_{\rm cross}$ ($\xi_M$ can be understood as a ``derived'' quantity from $\xi_{\rm cross}$ and CMI), and our main focus is precisely the case in which $\xi_M$ diverges while $\xi_{\rm cross} \sim a$.}.
This implies that there are only three relevant length scales: $l$, $l_B$, and $a$. 
Since CMI is dimensionless, one must have  
$\textrm{CMI}(l_B,l,a)=\textrm{CMI}(l_B/l,l_B/a)$.   
The continuum-surviving power-law CMI remains non-zero as $a \to 0$ at any fixed $l_B/l$. In contrast, if CMI decays algebraically with $l_B/a$ as well, the resulting power-law tail is cutoff-suppressed and vanishes in this limit. 
Nevertheless, at any fixed $a$ it remains algebraic in $l_B$ and therefore corresponds to an infinite Markov length. Thus, despite disappearing in the continuum scaling limit,
cutoff-suppressed CMI remains an obstruction to local 
reversibility at any fixed microscopic cutoff \footnote{More concretely, for a lattice-regularized system, the continuum limit can be realized by taking
the dimensionless lengths $n=l/a$ and
$n_B=l_B/a$ to infinity at fixed ratio
$y=n_B/n$. Thus, a contribution that decays only
algebraically with $n_B$ remains a power law at every
finite lattice spacing, despite vanishing in the continuum
limit.}.
This feature may be understood as $\xi_{\rm cross} \sim a $ being a ``dangerously irrelevant'' parameter for CMI. 
A similar dangerously irrelevant nature for ordinary (non-conditional) correlations in nonequilibrium systems has also been pointed out recently in Ref.\cite{metzger2026generic}.
We note that the above argument relies on  CMI being UV-finite, i.e., $\lim_{a\to 0} \text{CMI} < \infty$, which we  assume throughout the paper \footnote{This is known to be true for many ground states of local quantum field theories \cite{fursaev2006entanglement,ryu2006aspects,solodukhin2008entanglement,metlitski2009,Grover11_2,liu2013refinement,casini2015mutual,van2025finite,Holzhey94,calabrese2004entanglement,Levin06,vardhan2024petz,balasubramanian2019information}, and has also been verified for several nonequilibrium systems
\cite{chen2025local,chen2026constraints,chen2026renormalization}. It is also consistent with the numerical observation in Ref.~\cite{yang2025topological} that the CMI at the critical point of the dephased toric code is scale invariant.
We point out that a nonlocal spacetime action, such as the long-range Ising model considered here, does \emph{not} obstruct UV-finite CMI: a mixed state $\rho$ has UV-finite CMI if it admits a locally reversible RG with a fixed
on-site Hilbert-space dimension~\cite{chen2026renormalization}.}. As an aside, for a relevant perturbation, one can also consider the regime where $l \gg l_B, \xi_{\rm cross}$, and as discussed recently~\cite{chen2026constraints}, in this case an information-theoretic inequality for CMI provides a non-perturbative constraint on the RG flow.


