As we will discuss in detail below, a simple setting where
the aforementioned cutoff-suppressed CMI arises is Langevin dynamics whose unperturbed steady state is a critical Gibbs distribution of a finite-range local classical Hamiltonian, with power-law correlations
of local observables and power-law mutual information (MI)
between distant regions. The Markov property of such Gibbs distributions implies that $I(A:C|B)=0$ whenever $l_B$ is greater than the interaction range~\cite{clifford1971markov,leifer2008quantum,brown2012quantum}. We show that an irrelevant detailed-balance-breaking
perturbation of the dynamics generically produces non-universal
CMI with algebraic spatial decay. We further show that
the same distinction between genuine and cutoff-suppressed
power laws appears in the long-range Ising model. Here the critical point has genuine power-law CMI that survives the continuum limit, whereas the high-temperature paramagnetic phase, which flows to the infinite-temperature (trivial) state under RG \cite{fisher1972critical}, supports a cutoff-suppressed power-law CMI.

\textbf{One-dimensional CMI tails.} 
{We now present a simple yet powerful result for the scaling of CMI in one-dimensional systems.} 
Consider a translationally invariant one-dimensional mixed state $\rho$, which can be either classical or quantum, of infinite size close to the RG fixed point. 
Take $A$ and $C$ to be intervals of length $l$ separated by an interval $B$ of length $l_B$ [Fig.~\ref{fig:geometries}(b)].
{We now show that the CMI in this geometry decays faster than $l_B^{-2}$ if and only if the CMI vanishes as {the lattice cutoff} $a \to 0$.
This property, namely, $\lim_{a \to 0}\rm CMI = 0$, is what we mean by a fixed point being Markovian.
}

The essential point that leads to the claim is that, due to translational invariance, the CMI in Fig.\ref{fig:geometries}(b) is uniquely determined by the von Neumann entropy of an open interval $S(n)$ as $I(A:C|B) = 2S(n_B+n) - S(n_B) - S(n_B+2n)$, where we've introduced dimensionless variables $n_B = l_B/a$, $n = l/a$. 
A simple expansion when $n_B \gg n$  yields
$\text{CMI}(n,n_B) \simeq -n^2 d^2 S(n_B)/d n_B^2 = (n/n_B)^2 \times [-n_B^2 d^2 S(n_B)/d n_B^2]$ 
\footnote{We assume that the relative error is $o(1)$ in the joint limit $n_B\to\infty$ and $n/n_B\to0$.}
.
Reexpressing everything using  dimensionful variables, one finds that
\begin{equation}
\label{Eq:1d_cmi}
\textrm{CMI}(l,l_B, a)\simeq \Big(\frac{l}{l_B}\Big)^2 g\Big(\frac{l_B}{a}\Big).
\end{equation}
Therefore, the CMI (at fixed $l$ and $a$) decays faster than $l_B^{-2}$ if and only if $g(x)\to0$ as $x\to\infty$, which is  equivalent to the CMI vanishing as $a\to0$ at fixed $l$ and $l_B$.
This proves our aforementioned claim.
As an example, if $g(x) \sim x^{-\delta}$ with $\delta > 0$, one will obtain cutoff-suppressed CMI that decays as $l_B^{-(2+\delta)}$. 
We will later show that the high-temperature 1d long-range classical Ising model has such a scaling.

The above result constrains CMI of fixed points whose long-distance physics is spatially Markovian. Non-Markovian fixed points can  of course have CMI that does not satisfy this constraint. In particular, if CMI is UV finite and not cutoff-suppressed, i.e., it neither diverges nor vanishes as $a \to 0$, then  CMI $\sim (l/l_B)^2$. This conclusion has also been derived in Ref.~\cite{artiaco2025universal} assuming CMI is only a function of $l/l_B$. A few examples of such behavior are $(1+1)$-D conformal field 
theories, $(1+1)$-D directed percolation~\cite{chen2025local}, and a one-dimensional classical symmetry-breaking state under local decoherence studied in Refs.~\cite{lloyd2025diverging,chen2026constraints}.



Finally, the same argument implies that any power-law exponent $\eta<2$ would imply that $g(x)$ diverges when $x \to \infty$, and thus the CMI is not UV-finite.   
One example of this kind is the $s$-colored Motzkin chain~\cite{bravyi2012criticality,movassagh2016supercritical}, whose entanglement entropy for $s>1$ scales as $S(L)\sim L^{1/2}$~\cite{movassagh2016supercritical,movassagh2017entanglement}, implying $\eta=3/2$.






\textbf{{Cutoff-suppressed power-law} CMI from perturbing a critical Gibbs state.}  
We now present our main result: a generic detailed-balance-breaking perturbation of Langevin dynamics whose unperturbed steady state is a scale-invariant critical local Gibbs state induces a {cutoff-suppressed power-law CMI}.

Specifically, let $\phi(x,t)$ denote the field (or a collection of fields) and take
\begin{equation}
\label{Eq:langevin}
\partial_t \phi(x,t)=-\frac{\delta H [\phi]}{\delta\phi}+\epsilon\, v[\phi]+\zeta(x,t),
\end{equation}
where $H[\phi]$ is the local Hamiltonian, $\zeta(x,t)$ is Gaussian white noise with $\langle\zeta(x,t)\zeta(x',t')\rangle=2\delta^{d}(x-x')\delta(t-t')$, and $\epsilon v[\phi]$ is the perturbation that breaks detailed balance.
At $\epsilon=0$ the dynamics relaxes to the Gibbs distribution $P_0=e^{-H}/Z$. 
To compute the CMI to leading order in $\epsilon$, we combine the quadratic expansion of the CMI around a classical Markov distribution~\cite{kullback1962tests,kubkowski2021power} with the first-order McLennan--Zubarev expression for the steady state~\cite{mclennan1959statistical,zubarev1974nonequilibrium,maes2010rigorous}.
To state the result, let $X$ be any region with complement $\bar{X}$ and define $\langle O\rangle_{0|X}[\phi_X]
\equiv {\int\mathcal D\phi_{\bar X}\,e^{-H[\phi]}O[\phi]}/
{\int\mathcal D\phi_{\bar X}\,e^{-H[\phi]}}$, $\langle O\rangle_0\equiv\langle O\rangle_{0|\varnothing}$, and  $O_\times\equiv O-O_{AB}-O_{BC}+O_B$, with $O_X\equiv\langle O\rangle_{0|X}$. {Thus $O_X$ is the equilibrium expectation of $O$ conditioned on the field configuration in $X$, while $O_\times$ isolates the part of $O$ that cannot be captured by $AB$ and $BC$ separately.}
With this notation, we find that at the leading non-zero order in $\epsilon$ (see App.~\ref{app:perturbative_cmi} for details),
\begin{equation} 
\label{Eq:cmi_susceptibility} 
I_\epsilon(A:C|B)=\frac{\epsilon^2}{2}\int_0^\infty\!\!dt\int_0^\infty\!\!dt'\;
\big\langle (e^{t\mathcal{L}^{(0)}}\Sigma)_\times(e^{t'\mathcal{L}^{(0)}}\Sigma)_\times\big\rangle_0,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}^{(0)}=\int d^dx\left[\frac{\delta^2}{\delta\phi(\mathbf{x})^2}
-\frac{\delta H}{\delta\phi(\mathbf{x})}\frac{\delta}{\delta\phi(\mathbf{x})}\right]$ is the backward generator of the equilibrium dynamics and $\Sigma[\phi]=\int d^dx\left[v(\mathbf{x})\frac{\delta H}{\delta\phi(\mathbf{x})}
-\frac{\delta v(\mathbf{x})}{\delta\phi(\mathbf{x})}\right]$ is the source generated by the perturbation.   Although our derivation above used additive white noise, Eq.~\eqref{Eq:cmi_susceptibility} holds more generally for local noise that preserves detailed balance with $P_0$, upon replacing $\mathcal{L}^{(0)}$ by the appropriate unperturbed generator.


Using Eq.\eqref{Eq:cmi_susceptibility}, we now argue that a generic detailed-balance-breaking perturbation from a critical local Gibbs state with algebraically decaying mutual information will induce algebraically decaying CMI.  

Being an irrelevant perturbation implies that $\xi_{\rm cross} =  \epsilon^{1/|\omega|}$ (and hence  $\epsilon^2  = \xi_{\rm cross}^{2 |\omega|}$) with  $\omega <0$ the RG eigenvalue. 
On the other hand, the double integrals in Eq.\eqref{Eq:cmi_susceptibility} are completely determined by the {unperturbed} local Gibbs state, which we assume to be scale invariant, and thus the double integrals can only depend on the shape of the tripartition and three length scales $l_B$, $l$, and $a$.
In the scaling limit $l, l_B \gg a$, one then finds
\begin{equation} 
\label{Eq:cmi_critical}
\text{CMI}={\frac{1}{2}}\left(\frac{\xi_{\rm cross}}{l_B}\right)^{2|\omega|} \Phi(l_B/l),
\end{equation}
where $\Phi(l_B/l) = l_B^{2|\omega|}\int_0^\infty\!\!dt\int_0^\infty\!\!dt'\;
\big\langle (e^{t\mathcal{L}^{(0)}}\Sigma)_\times(e^{t'\mathcal{L}^{(0)}}\Sigma)_\times\big\rangle_0$ up to corrections that vanish  as $a \to 0$. 
One expects that for a generic detailed-balance-breaking perturbation, the operators $(e^{t\mathcal{L}^{(0)}}\Sigma)_\times$ have a nonzero overlap with scaling operators of the critical fixed point. Due to scale invariance, the corresponding correlators decay as powers of distance. Integrating these correlators over regions of size $l$ and $l_B$ then produces powers of their ratio, and thus $\Phi(y)$ is expected to decay as a power of $y$ in the $y \gg 1$ (i.e., $l_B\gg l$) limit. Therefore, the Markov length diverges. 

{As a concrete application, consider the Ising-symmetric dynamical critical point of Toom's cellular automata~\cite{toom1974nonergodic,toom1980stable}.}
Following Ref.~\cite{he1990generic}, the Toom-like dynamics can be described by the Langevin equation of Eq.\eqref{Eq:langevin} with the Hamiltonian being the $\phi^4$ theory $H=\int d^2x\,[\tfrac{1}{2}(\nabla\phi)^2+\tfrac{1}{2}m^2\phi^2+\tfrac{u}{4!}\phi^4]$ and $v_{\rm toom}[\phi] = (\partial_x^3+\partial_y^3)\phi$.
Since $v_{\rm toom}[\phi]$ has three spatial derivatives, this perturbation is expected to be irrelevant at the unperturbed  dynamical fixed point, which corresponds to the standard model-A~\cite{grinstein1985statistical}. Using the scaling analysis of Ref.~\cite{shinkai1992bistability} together with the numerical results in Ref.~\cite{Godreche2017generic}, we estimate $\omega \approx -1.3 \pm 0.2$. 
We therefore predict that the Ising-symmetric critical point of Toom's cellular automaton exhibits cutoff-suppressed power-law CMI.

We note that while here we expand CMI only up to $O(\epsilon^2)$, essentially the same argument for a divergent Markov length goes through in higher-order expansions.

{Above we showed that a generic detailed-balance-breaking perturbation to the dynamical fixed point of a critical, {classical} Gibbs state leads to cutoff-suppressed power-law CMI. A useful sufficient condition for \textit{excluding} such CMI, at least at the leading order (Eq.\eqref{Eq:cmi_susceptibility}), is rapid mixing together with locality of the unperturbed dynamics.  By rapid mixing we mean that there is a system-size-independent relaxation rate $\gamma>0$ such that any zero-mean observable $O$ satisfies $\|e^{t\mathcal L^{(0)}}O\|_{2,0}\le e^{-\gamma t}\|O\|_{2,0}$, where $\|O\|_{2,0}^2\equiv\langle O^2\rangle_0$.  Locality implies that if $O_X$ is initially supported in a region $X$, then after time $t$ it can be approximated by an observable supported within distance $R$ of $X$, with an error that is exponentially small when $R$ is larger than the distance information can propagate in time $t$; schematically, $\|e^{t\mathcal L^{(0)}}O_X-O_{X_R}(t)\|_{2,0}\lesssim e^{vt-\mu R}$, where $O_{X_R}(t)$ is supported within distance $R$ of $X$, and $v,\mu>0$.  Applying this to the local terms in $\Sigma$ in Eq.~\eqref{Eq:cmi_susceptibility}, the $\times$ projection can only retain contributions that have propagated across the buffer, and therefore $\|(e^{t\mathcal L^{(0)}}\Sigma)_\times\|_{2,0}\lesssim\min\{e^{-\gamma t},e^{vt-\mu l_B}\}$.  Integrating over $t$ in $V=\int_0^\infty dt\,e^{t\mathcal L^{(0)}}\Sigma$ then gives $\|V_\times\|_{2,0}\lesssim e^{-\kappa l_B}$ with $\kappa=\mu\gamma/(v+\gamma)>0$.  Hence Eq.~\eqref{Eq:cmi_susceptibility} gives $I(A:C|B)\lesssim \epsilon^2 e^{-2\kappa l_B}$ at leading order. This condition also suggests mechanisms other than the critical slowing down for generating cutoff-suppressed power-law CMI. For example, conserved dynamics need not be rapidly mixing: even when the steady state itself has short-ranged correlations, long-wavelength fluctuations can relax diffusively, so the time integral in Eq.~\eqref{Eq:cmi_susceptibility} can generate cutoff-suppressed power-law mutual information (MI)~\cite{grinstein1991generic,damman2024algebraic,metzger2026generic} and CMI.  We give an explicit example below.} 



\textbf{General CMI scaling for Gaussian systems.}
Above we derived a perturbative expression for cutoff-suppressed power-law CMI in critical Gibbs states perturbed by a detailed-balance-breaking term. Since this is a general phenomenon, it is instructive to demonstrate it non-perturbatively in solvable models. To that end, we derive the scaling of the CMI for a broad class of Gaussian theories in which a local critical kernel is supplemented by an irrelevant nonlocal contribution.
Let us first recall that a translationally invariant Gaussian distribution is fully specified by its quadratic kernel $K(\mathbf q)$ as 
$P_{}[\phi]\propto\exp[-\frac{1}{2}\int_{\mathbf q\neq0}
\phi(\mathbf q)K(\mathbf q)\phi(-\mathbf q)]$, 
where $\int_{\mathbf q}\equiv\int d^d q/(2\pi)^d$.
Being a nonlocal Gibbs distribution here simply means that $K(\mathbf q)$ is nonanalytic as $\mathbf q \to 0$, as local terms are finite-derivative contact 
operators and thus are all analytic after Fourier transformation.

Consider the scenario where $K$ can be decomposed into a local scale-invariant part $K^{\rm loc}$ with $K^{\rm loc}(\lambda\mathbf q)=\lambda^{h_{\rm loc}}K^{\rm loc}(\mathbf q)$ and an irrelevant nonlocal part with $K^{\rm nloc}(\lambda\mathbf q)=\lambda^{h_{\rm nloc}}K^{\rm nloc}(\mathbf q)$.
Specifically,
\begin{equation}
\label{Eq:precision_two_term}
K(\mathbf q;\xi_{\rm cross})=K^{\rm loc}(\mathbf q)
+\xi_{\rm cross}^{|\omega|}K^{\rm nloc}(\mathbf q),
\end{equation}
where $\omega = h_{\rm loc} - h_{\rm nloc}$ is the scaling dimension of the nonlocal fixed point, which we assume to be \emph{irrelevant}, i.e., $\omega = -|\omega|<0$.
For a local critical Gaussian fixed point, $h_{\rm loc}=2$, which comes from the $\nabla^2$ term in the action. 
We note that $\xi_{\rm cross}$ in Eq.\eqref{Eq:precision_two_term} ensures that $K^{\rm loc}$ and $\xi_{\rm cross}^{|\omega|} K^{\rm nloc}$ carry the same dimension. 

In the End Matter, we present a simple scaling argument for the CMI in the geometry of Fig.~\ref{fig:geometries}(a), independent of further details of $K^{\rm loc}$ and $K^{\rm nloc}$. 
For $l_B \gg l$, the argument gives the following CMI scaling forms in three regimes distinguished by their relation to $\xi_{\rm cross}$: 
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
    \item When $\xi_{\rm cross}\ll l\ll l_B$, which is the regime where Eq.\eqref{Eq:cmi_susceptibility} applies, one finds
    \begin{equation}
\label{Eq:large_y_regime_a}
\text{CMI} \sim \Big(\frac{\xi_{\rm cross}}{l_B } \Big)^{2|\omega|}  \Big(\frac{l}{l_B } \Big)^{d+h_{\rm loc}}.
\end{equation}
This supports the perturbative result in Eq.\eqref{Eq:cmi_critical}, where the $\xi_{\rm cross}/l_B$ dependence is determined by the irrelevant eigenvalue while the $l/l_B$ dependence is determined by the local theory.
    \item When $l\ll\xi_{\rm cross}\ll l_B$, one finds that the exponent for $l_B/l$ now depends on the \emph{nonlocal} scale-invariant theory as
\begin{equation}
\label{Eq:large_y_regime_b}
\text{CMI} \sim \Big(\frac{\xi_{\rm cross}}{l_B } \Big)^{|\omega|}\,\Big(\frac{l}{l_B } \Big)^{d+h_{\rm nloc}} .
\end{equation}
\item Finally, when $l\ll l_B\ll\xi_{\rm cross}$, one finds
\begin{equation}
\label{Eq:large_y_regime_c}
\text{CMI} \sim \Big(\frac{l}{l_B } \Big)^{d+h_{\rm nloc}},
\end{equation}
which is consistent with the intuition that if $\xi_{\rm cross}$ is greater than all the length scales at which we probe the system, then one will observe the scale-invariant behavior associated with the non-local kernel (``UV fixed point'').
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{An exactly solvable two-component Toom-like model.}
As a concrete example, we now discuss a Gaussian model perturbed with the Toom-like detailed-balance-breaking term $\mathcal{D}_3 \phi = (\partial_x^3+\partial_y^3)\phi$. 

To keep the state Gaussian while {rendering} $K(\mathbf q)$ nonanalytic, we introduce a second field and give the two fields opposite spatial anisotropies, which is a bit similar to the role of anisotropy played in Ref.\cite{grinstein1990conservation}.
Specifically, we collect two real fields in $d = 2$ as $\Phi=(\phi,\psi)$, and then consider the Langevin equation 
\begin{equation}
\label{Eq:two_component}
\begin{aligned}
& \partial_t\Phi  =-(S+A)\Phi+\zeta,\\
& S  =-\begin{pmatrix}\partial_x^2+c\partial_y^2&0\\0&c\partial_x^2+\partial_y^2\end{pmatrix},\
A =-\epsilon(\partial^3_x+\partial^3_y)\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation} 
with $c>0$, $c\neq1$, and $\zeta(x,t)$ Gaussian white noise with $\langle\zeta(x,t)\zeta(x',t')\rangle=2  \delta^{2}(x-x')\delta(t-t')$.   

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering

\caption{
    CMI of the model in
Eq.~\eqref{Eq:two_component} on a periodic $128\times128$ lattice with
$c=2$ and a single-site region $A$ ($l=1$). All lengths are in lattice units. Main: CMI versus $l_B$ for several
$\xi_{\rm cross}=\epsilon$.
Inset: CMI$\,(l_B/l)^4$ versus $l_B/\xi_{\rm cross}$.   
}
\label{fig:two_component_numerics}
\end{figure}     

Two {consequences} follow. First, $S \Phi = \delta H/\delta \Phi$ with $H$ the critical Gaussian
Hamiltonian $H=\frac12\int d^2x\,[(\partial_x\phi)^2+c(\partial_y\phi)^2+c(\partial_x\psi)^2+(\partial_y\psi)^2]$. Therefore, the steady state when $\epsilon = 0$ is a local Gibbs state that has infinite correlation length (but zero Markov length).
Second, the second term $A \Phi$ is antisymmetric, and thus it breaks detailed balance.  
At the Gaussian fixed point $z=2$, so $\omega = z-3 = -1$, and thus $\xi_{\rm cross}=\epsilon$. 
Since $h_{\rm loc} = 2$, without knowing any details about $K_{\rm nloc}$, one can already predict from Eq.\eqref{Eq:large_y_regime_a} that generically, $\text{CMI} \sim (\xi_{\rm cross}/{l_B})^{2 |\omega|} ({l}/{l_B})^{d+ h_{\text{loc}}} =(\xi_{\rm cross}/{l_B})^{2 }({l}/{l_B})^{4} $.


We now numerically verify the predicted dependence on $l_B$ and $\epsilon$ by evaluating the CMI with the geometry in Fig.\ref{fig:geometries}(a) from {the exact steady-state}. 
We set $c=2$, take $A$ to be a single site ($l=1$), and consider how the CMI scales as a function of $l_B$, measuring lengths in lattice units. In practice, one has to keep the whole system finite, so we consider a periodic $128\times128$ lattice.  
Fig.~\ref{fig:two_component_numerics} shows the predicted $l_B^{-6}$ decay 
and the collapse of $I(A:C|B)\,(l_B/l)^4$ versus $l_B/\xi_{\rm cross}$ onto
a curve of slope $-2$. 
Further numerical details are given in App.~\ref{app:two_component_exact}.

As hinted earlier, even when the unperturbed steady state has short-ranged correlations, slow relaxation dynamics can generate cutoff-suppressed power-law CMI. Keeping $S$ and $A$ in Eq.~\eqref{Eq:two_component} unchanged, consider
instead diagonal conserved noise with momentum-space covariance
$D(\mathbf k)=\mathrm{diag}\!\left[T_\phi(k_x^2+c k_y^2),
T_\psi(c k_x^2+k_y^2)\right]$, where $T_\phi, T_\psi$ are arbitrary noise strengths with $T_\phi \neq T_\psi$. At $\epsilon=0$ the stationary distribution is simply	$P_0[\phi,\psi]\propto
\exp\left[-\frac12\int d^2x
\left(\frac{\phi^2}{T_\phi}+\frac{\psi^2}{T_\psi}\right)\right]$ and thus fields at distinct spatial points are independent, and the MI and CMI {vanish identically}. Nevertheless, the same
Toom-like perturbation generates the off-diagonal elements of the quadratic kernel, 
$\delta K_{\phi\psi}(\mathbf k)
=
\frac{i\epsilon}{1+c}
\left(\frac{1}{T_\phi}-\frac{1}{T_\psi}\right)
\frac{k_x^3+k_y^3}{k_x^2+k_y^2}
+O(\epsilon^2)$,
and one may verify it produces cutoff-suppressed power-law CMI (as well as MI).



\textbf{Long-range Ising model.}
Having discussed a few examples of nonuniversal power-law tails, we close with a classic example where one can tune a parameter to access the crossover between universal and non-universal power-law tails: the 
long-range Ising model in $d$ spatial dimensions, with $\rho \propto \exp(-H)$ and the Hamiltonian   
\begin{equation}
\label{Eq:long_range_ising}
H=-\frac{\beta}{2}\sum_{i\neq j}\frac{s_i s_j}{|\mathbf{r}_i-\mathbf{r}_j|^{d+\sigma}},
\end{equation}
where $s_i = \pm 1$ denotes the Ising spin. 
We briefly discuss how this example illustrates the generic 1d result and the CMI scaling for Gaussian systems, with details collected in App.\ref{app:lr_ising}.

Let us first consider the irrelevant CMI tails.
For any $d$ and $\sigma>0$, for sufficiently small $\beta$, the long-range interaction is irrelevant and the system flows to a trivial product state \footnote{When $\sigma <0$, the aligned-state energy per spin diverges in the thermodynamic limit, implying that the Gibbs state at any $\beta>0$ is essentially $[(|0\rangle \langle 0|)^{N} + (|1\rangle \langle 1|)^{N}]/2 $, which has zero CMI.  
}. 
Therefore, our 1d result discussed in Eq.\eqref{Eq:1d_cmi} predicts that the CMI tail $l_B^{-\eta}$ with the geometry in Fig.\ref{fig:geometries}(b) has exponent  $\eta >2$.
Indeed, a standard high-temperature expansion in App.\ref{app:lr_ising} shows that $\eta = 2(1+\sigma)>2$ for any $\sigma>0$.

To make analytical progress close to the critical point, we consider the Gaussian regime $0<\sigma<d/2$ so that the physics is captured by a quadratic kernel of the form $K(\mathbf q)=\mu+|\mathbf{q}|^\sigma$, where $\mu = 0$ corresponds to the critical inverse temperature $\beta = \beta_c$ \cite{fisher1972critical}.  
When $d=1$, we find that the CMI in the geometry of Fig.~\ref{fig:geometries}(b) can be computed analytically at $\mu = 0$, and in the $l_B\gg l$ limit, we obtain $\text{CMI} = \sigma^2(l/l_B)^2/8+O((l/l_B)^3)$ (see App.~\ref{app:lr_ising}).
Therefore, the CMI has a universal tail with $\eta = 2$ that survives the continuum limit, reflecting the nonlocality of the critical Gaussian theory. 
{More generally, in any spatial dimension, Eqs.\eqref{Eq:large_y_regime_a}-\eqref{Eq:large_y_regime_c}, with $h_{\rm loc} = 0$, $h_{\rm nloc} = \sigma$ (and thus $|\omega| = |h_{\rm loc} - h_{\rm nloc}| =\sigma$), describe the crossover from genuine power-law CMI at the critical point ($\xi_{\rm cross }= \infty$) to cutoff-suppressed power-law CMI in the high-temperature phase at distances $l_B \gg \xi_{\rm cross }$.} 
We numerically verify Eqs.\eqref{Eq:large_y_regime_b}-\eqref{Eq:large_y_regime_c} in $d = 2$ (see App.~\ref{app:lr_ising}). 


\textbf{Discussion.}
We have shown that the Markov length can diverge in systems whose universal physics is Markovian. 
{This exposes a tension between the conventional RG notion of universality, in which states flowing to the same long-distance fixed point are regarded as equivalent~\cite{cardy1996scaling}, and channel-based notions of mixed-state phase equivalence~\cite{sang2025stability,sang2025mixed,sang2024mixed}.
On that note, channels of polylogarithmic range cannot connect a state with power-law CMI to one with exponentially decaying CMI through a locally reversible path~\cite{jinmin2026universal}, 
whereas a concrete example shows that channels of sublinear range can~\cite{chen2026renormalization}.
It is then natural to wonder if one should relax the channel range in mixed-state phase equivalence from polylogarithmic~\cite{sang2025mixed} to sublinear~\cite{sang2024mixed} in the system size. 
 Alternatively, one may consider enlarging mixed-state phase equivalence to include short-depth channels with algebraic spatial tails, provided the strength of these long-range terms is itself cutoff-suppressed.}

One concrete prediction of our result is a cutoff-suppressed power-law CMI tail at the critical point on Toom's symmetric line, despite its expected Markovian universal physics~\cite{grinstein1985statistical,he1987numerical,Godreche2017generic}.
Notably, the exponent of such a power-law tail is universal and encodes the irrelevant RG eigenvalue. Apart from Toom's model, several cellular automata have been constructed that can stabilize classical memory without symmetry~\cite{pajouheshgar2026exploring,lake2025squeezing}. 
It would be interesting to study the CMI in these models, as their exponents differ from those of Model~A and may lead to the genuinely power-law CMI that survives the continuum limit.

Finally, we would like to highlight an upcoming work~\cite{negari2026}, which constructs one-dimensional examples where an arbitrarily weak local quantum channel generates power-law CMI, $I(A:C|B)\sim l_B^{-\eta}$ with $\eta>2$, and hence an infinite Markov length at any nonzero channel strength.  This provides another realization of
cutoff-suppressed power-law CMI in a rather different setting. 

